// Source excerpts from "The Story of the Union" guide, per chapter.
// Used ONLY as grounding material when the app asks Groq to generate extra practice questions.
window.SOURCES = {
  1: 'CHAPTER 1  How Enemies Built a Union The story of how a single idea about coal and steel turned a continent of war into the longest peace it has ever known. T here is a room on the Quai d\'Orsay in Paris called the Salon de l\'Horloge, the Clock Room, and on the afternoon of 9 May 1950 it was crowded with journ‐ alists who had been summoned at short notice and told almost nothing. A French foreign minister named Robert Schuman stood up and read a short statement. Most of the reporters did not understand, at first, that they were listening to one of the most consequential ideas of the twentieth century. It did not sound consequential. It was about coal and steel. To understand why that quiet announcement mattered, you have to stand where those journalists stood and see what they saw when they walked outside. A continent that had tried everything else Europe in 1950 was a place recovering from suicide. In the space of a single lifetime it had fought two world wars that began as European quarrels and then dragged in the planet. France and Germany alone had gone to war three times in seventy years, in 1870, in 1914 and in 1939, and the third time had ended with cities reduced to brick dust and a death toll that ran into the tens of millions. Anyone reading Schuman\'s statement that afternoon had lived through at least one of those wars as an adult, and probably two. The instinct after a war is to punish the loser and guard against the next round. That instinct had been tried after 1918, when the Treaty of Versailles squeezed Germany, and everyone in that room knew how it had ended: in resentment, in economic collapse, and eventually in Hitler. So the interesting question in 1950 was not how to keep Germany down. It was the opposite. West Germany was recovering fast, the Cold War had already begun, and the only real choice was whether it came back as a rival, the way it had before, or as something entirely new. The man who did not believe in grand plans The idea did not really belong to Schuman. It belonged to a man who had stood quietly to one side of European politics for most of his life, a cognac merchant\'s son named Jean Monnet. Monnet had spent both world wars organizing supplies between allies, and the experience had taught him something most politicians never learn: that soaring speeches about the brotherhood of nations change nothing, and that people cooperate not because they are told to love one another but because they are quietly tied together until pulling apart becomes more painful than staying. He had no time for grand constitutions written in advance. He believed in concrete steps that create real, practical solidarity, one small irreversible fact at a time. Nations cooperate not because they are told to love one another, but because they are tied togeth‐ er until pulling apart hurts more than staying. So instead of proposing a United States of Europe, which would have been voted down within a week, Monnet proposed the smallest possible first step that could not be undone. He picked the two things every war in living memory had been fought with: coal and steel. Steel to build the tanks and the guns. Coal to fire the furnaces that made the steel. Control those two industries and you control a nation\'s capacity to wage war. His idea was to place French and German coal and steel under a single shared authority, so that neither country could ever again quietly build the machinery of war without the other seeing it and sharing in it. War between France and Ger‐ many, not merely unthinkable, but materially impossible. Schuman, the foreign minister, took the risk of putting his name to it. That is why the world remembers it as the Schuman Declara‐ tion, delivered on 9 May 1950, and why 9 May is still celebrated every year as Europe Day. The first surrender of sovereignty The declaration became a treaty with remarkable speed. The Treaty of Paris, signed on 18 April 1951 and in force on 23 July 1952, created the European Coal and Steel Community, the ECSC. Six countries signed it, and these six are the founders of everything that followed. Learn them as a group, because they recur throughout this whole story: France, West Germany, Italy, Belgi‐ um, the Netherlands and Luxembourg. Historians simply call them the Six. Notice something about that list. Five years after the end of the war, defeated Germany sat at the table not as a defendant but as an equal partner. That was the whole point. The ECSC also introduced a genuinely radical invention. It was run by a body called the High Authority, and the countries agreed its decisions over their coal and steel would be binding on them, even against their own governments\' wishes. For the first time in modern history, sovereign nations handed a slice of their own power upward to a shared institution standing above them. Monnet became its first president. That High Authority is the direct ancestor of today\'s European Commission, and that single idea, that member states would obey a common authority they had created together, is the seed of everything the Union is. The dream that was too big, too soon Flushed with success, the Six immediately tried the same trick with the most sensitive thing a nation owns: its army. In the early 1950s they drew up a treaty for a European Defence Community, a shared European army with German soldiers in it, less than a decade after German soldiers had occupied Paris. It was a step too far, too fast. In 1954 the French National As‐ sembly, the very parliament of the country that had proposed it, refused to ratify it, and the European army collapsed before it was born. The failure taught the founders a lesson they never forgot. When they aimed straight at the heart of national pride, defence, they were thrown back. So they returned to Monnet\'s method: forget the heart, go for the wallet. Tie the economies together first, so tightly and so profitably that political union would one day follow almost by accident. For the next forty years, Europe was built through trade, not troops. Rome, and the Common Market The proof came quickly. On 25 March 1957, on Rome\'s Capit‐ oline Hill, the Six signed the Treaties of Rome, in force on 1 January 1958. There were two. One created Euratom, to pool nuclear research. The other, far more important, created the European Economic Community, the EEC, whose ambition was simple to state and enormous to achieve: to tear down the customs barriers between the six countries and merge them into one com‐ mon market, a single trading space where goods, and eventually people, money and services, could move as freely as they did within any one country. This is where the everyday, prosperous, trading Europe was born. The general who said no No story of building anything is complete without the person who wanted to build it differently, and Europe\'s was Charles de Gaulle, the towering, prickly president of France. He believed in Europe but not in the supranational part, the part where a shared authority could overrule a proud nation. In 1965 the tension boiled over. Angry at proposals to strengthen the community\'s central power, France simply stopped showing up. For roughly six months French chairs sat empty at the table, a standoff history remembers as the empty chair crisis.',
  2: 'CHAPTER 2  The Creed and the Colours What the Union says it stands for, the flag and anthem that fly above it, and the price a member pays for ceasing to believe. T here is a question every candidate for an EU job is eventually asked, in one form or another, and it is never really about treaty dates or trade statistics. It is some version of a much simpler question: do you be‐ lieve in this? Because the European Union is a strange kind of club. Most international organisations will take you if you are the right sort of country in the right part of the world. The Union will not. You cannot buy your way in, and being European is not enough. You have to sign up to a creed. This chapter is about that creed, about the things it commits the Union to do, and about the flag, the anthem and the motto that fly above it all. It is also about what happens to a member that stops believing. The six words that hold the Union together Article 2 of the Treaty on European Union is a single short paragraph, and it is the moral spine of the entire project. It declares that the Union is founded on respect for human dignity, free‐ dom, democracy, equality, the rule of law, and respect for human rights, including the rights of persons belonging to minorities. Six ideas. Learn them, because "which of the following is a value of the Union" is one of the most reliable questions in the whole exam, and the trap is always a plausible near-synonym that is not on the list. THE CREED AND THE COLOURS Take them one at a time, because each was chosen against a memory. Human dignity comes first on purpose: it is the floor beneath everything, the insistence that a human being is never merely a means to someone else\'s end. Freedom and democracy answer the dictatorships that twice dragged the continent into ruin; democracy is simply government that answers to the gov‐ erned. Equality and human rights were written by people who had watched rights stripped away by law before they were stripped away by force. And the explicit mention of the rights of persons belonging to minorities is not decoration either, because Europe\'s darkest chapters were written first against its minorities. There is a second sentence to Article 2, and the exam sometimes blurs the two. After listing the six values, it adds that these are common to the Member States in a society in which pluralism, non-discrimination, tolerance, justice, solidarity and equality between women and men prevail. Keep the halves straight: the first list is the values; the second describes the kind of society those values are meant to produce. Human dignity ARTICLE 2 · TEU The rule of law FOUNDED — ARTICLE 2 TEU You cannot buy your way into the Union, and being European is not enough. You have to sign up to a creed. Why the creed has teeth Fine words are cheap. What makes Article 2 more than a poster on the wall are two other articles that turn belief into consequence: one is a door, the other is a lever. The door is Article 49. It says that any European State which respects the values of Article 2, and is committed to promoting them, may apply to join the Union. Read that carefully: the values are literally the entry ticket. This is where the famous Copenhagen criteria come from, the checklist agreed at the Copenhagen THE CREED AND THE COLOURS summit in 1993 that every candidate must satisfy: stable institu‐ tions guaranteeing democracy and the rule of law; a functioning market economy able to cope with competition; and the capacity to take on the obligations of membership, the whole body of existing EU law known as the acquis. A country does not join the Union by being rich or nearby. It joins by proving it shares the creed. The lever is Article 7, and it answers the harder question: what happens when a country, already inside, drifts away from the values it signed up to? Article 7 lets the Union act against a member for a serious breach of Article 2, and at its sharpest it can suspend that country\'s rights, including its vote in the Council of Ministers. It is nicknamed the nuclear option, and it was built to be hard to fire: the gravest step requires the other members to agree almost unanimously, which means one friendly government can shield another from punishment. In recent years the Union has reached for these tools amid bitter disputes over judicial independence and the rule of law. The specific cases belong to the current-affairs chapter, but the principle is what matters here: in the European Union, the creed is enforceable. What the Union is actually for If Article 2 is what the Union believes, Article 3 is its to-do list, the aims it commits itself to pursue. It is longer and far less quotable, but a handful of its objectives surface again and again. The Union sets out to promote peace, its values and the well- being of its peoples. It commits to an area of freedom, security and justice without internal borders, and to a single internal mar‐ ket. It promises sustainable development, balanced economic growth and price stability, built on what the treaty calls a highly competitive social market economy aiming at full employment and social progress. That phrase, social market economy, is worth memorising: it is the Union\'s chosen economic model, a capitalism paired with a strong social safety net, and it is a favourite of examiners. Article 3 also promises a high level of environmental protection, the fight against social exclusion and discrimination, economic, social and territorial cohesion and solidarity among Member States, respect for the Union\'s rich cultural and linguist‐ ic diversity, and an economic and monetary union whose cur‐ rency is the euro. Finally, in its dealings with the wider world, the Union pledges to uphold and promote its values. Read the list twice and you notice the trick of it: nearly every objective in Article 3 is simply a value from Article 2 turned into a job of work. The colours: the flag Now the symbols, beginning with the one everybody thinks they already understand. The European flag is a circle of twelve gold stars on a blue field, and the single most common mistake in the world about it is to assume those twelve stars stand for twelve member states. They do not, and they never have. The flag was designed when the Communities had only six members. It was adopted with twelve stars, and it kept exactly THE CREED AND THE COLOURS twelve when the membership climbed to fifteen, then twenty-five, then twenty-eight, and it still has twelve today with twenty-seven. The number was never a headcount. Twelve was chosen as an old symbol of completeness and perfection, the twelve months, the twelve hours of the clock, and the ring itself, a circle with no beginning and no end, stands for unity and harmony among the peoples of Europe. There is a second surprise hidden in the flag. It is not originally a European Union flag at all. It was created for the Council of Europe, that other Strasbourg body you met in Chapter 1, and adopted by it in 1955. The European Communities only took it as their own in 1985. So the flag is older than the Union that flies it, and its stars have nothing whatever to do with counting members. The twelve stars have never counted the members. Twelve is completeness, and the ring has no beginning and no end. The anthem, the motto, and the day The Union\'s other symbols each carry a small surprise of their own.',
  3: 'CHAPTER 3  A New Legal Order How the Union makes law, why a regulation is not a directive, and how a court in Luxembourg quietly placed European law above the law of nations. A treaty, normally, is a promise between governments. It binds the states that sign it and nobody else. If two countries agree to lower their tariffs, that is a matter for their two governments; it is not something a shopkeeper in one of them can wave in front of a judge. That is how international law had always worked. So here is the puzzle at the heart of this chapter. The European Union began as a set of treaties between governments, and yet today its law reaches straight past the governments to ordinary people. A company can rely on it in its own national court. It overrides laws that national parliaments have passed. A regulation drafted in Brussels becomes, the moment it takes effect, the law of the land in twenty-seven countries at once, without a single national parliament lifting a finger. How did a bundle of promises between states turn into that? The answer is one of the quietest and most consequential revolutions in modern legal history, and it happened, appropriately enough, in a courtroom in Luxembourg. The foundations: primary law Start at the top, with the law from which all the rest flows. At the summit of the European legal order sit two great treaties of exactly equal rank. The first is the Treaty on European Union, the TEU. Think of it as the who and why: it sets out the Union\'s values, its objectives, its institutions in outline, and its foreign and security A NEW LEGAL ORDER policy. The second is the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, the TFEU, the far longer what and how: the de‐ tailed policies, the powers, the machinery of decision-making. The two are not ranked one above the other. The treaties themselves state that they have the same legal value, and together they are the closest thing the Union has to a constitution. Since the Treaty of Lisbon, a third text stands alongside them. The Charter of Fundamental Rights was given, in the words of the TEU, the same legal value as the Treaties. So the rights it protects are not a wish-list; they are binding law at the very top of the pile. These texts, together with the general principles the Court has drawn from them, are what lawyers call primary law. Everything else the Union does must obey it. HIGHER RANK · PREVAILS PRIMARY LAW TEU · TFEU · Charter · principles INTERNATIONAL AGREEMENTS SECONDARY LAW Regulations · Directives · Decisions · Recommendations · Opinions How the Union actually makes law Below the treaties sits the law the institutions produce day to day, secondary law, and here you meet the single most important, most tested distinction in the whole of EU knowledge. The toolbox is defined in one famous article, Article 288 TFEU, which lists ex‐ actly five kinds of act. Three of them bind. Two of them do not. The most powerful is the regulation. A regulation is binding in its entirety and directly applicable in all Member States. Read that slowly, because every word does work. Binding in its en‐ tirety means all of it, not just the goal. Directly applicable means it becomes law automatically, the instant it enters into force, with no need for any national parliament to pass anything. One text, drafted once, lands identically in twenty-seven countries on the same day. When the Union wants a single, uniform rule to mean exactly the same thing from Lisbon to Helsinki, it reaches for a regulation. This is why Europe\'s data-protection rulebook was written as a regulation: the same rules, word for word, everywhere. The directive works in a completely different way, and confusing the two is the classic error the exam is built to catch. A directive is binding as to the result to be achieved, but it leaves each country free to choose the form and methods of getting there. A directive is not law you obey directly; it is an instruction to each Member State to make its own law that reaches a common European goal, by a set deadline. This process is called transposi‐ tion. So one directive produces twenty-seven national laws, all aiming at the same target but each written in the local legal style. A regulation imposes uniformity; a directive harmonises toward a shared result while leaving room for national difference. Remember it as a single line: a regulation applies, a directive must be trans‐ posed. A NEW LEGAL ORDER Identical in all 27 states 27 different national laws directly applicable · no transposition each transposed · by a deadline APPLIES, A DIRECTIVE MUST BE TRANSPOSED The third binding act is the decision. A decision is binding in its entirety, and where it names those it is addressed to, it binds only them. It is the Union\'s instrument for the specific rather than the general: a ruling aimed at one company, one country, one situation. The last two acts carry no legal force at all. A recommendation suggests. An opinion expresses a view. Neither obliges anyone to do anything. They are what lawyers call soft law, influential per‐ haps, but never binding. If a question asks which act is not binding, the answer lives here. A regulation applies. A directive must be transposed. Everything about the exam\'s favourite trap is contained in that one sen‐ tence. The quiet revolution Now return to the puzzle we started with, because none of what makes this a genuine legal order, rather than a grand international agreement, is actually written in the treaties. It was built, case by case, by the Court of Justice in Luxembourg, in two judgments of the early 1960s that between them changed everything. The first came in 1963, in a case called Van Gend en Loos. A Dutch importer was charged a customs duty on a shipment of industrial adhesive, a higher duty than the Treaty of Rome was supposed to allow. An ordinary trade dispute over a few guilders. But the Court used it to announce something extraordinary. European law, it said, was a new legal order, one whose subjects were not only the Member States but also their nationals, indi‐ viduals who could therefore rely on that law directly in their own national courts. This is the principle of direct effect: European law does not stop at the government\'s door; it can create rights that ordinary people are entitled to enforce. The second came a year later, in 1964, in Costa v ENEL. An Italian lawyer refused to pay an electricity bill worth a handful of lire, arguing that the law nationalising the electricity industry clashed with the Treaty. Another trivial dispute. But it forced the Court to answer the deepest question of all: when European law and a national law collide, which one wins? The Court\'s answer was unequivocal. European law prevails, even over a national law passed later. The logic was inescapable: if each country could override European law with its own statutes whenever it pleased, then European law would mean something different in every A NEW LEGAL ORDER country, and the common project would dissolve. This is the principle of primacy. Together, direct effect and primacy are the twin pillars on which the entire edifice stands. There is a refinement worth knowing: directives, unlike regulations, do not automatically reach individuals, but once a transposition deadline has passed a citizen may still rely on an untransposed directive against the state itself, though not against another private party.',
  4: 'CHAPTER 4  Powers on Loan Why the Union can only do what its members have let it do, the three kinds of competence, and the rule that keeps power as close to the citizen as possible. A national government, in principle, can legislate on almost anything. It can raise an army, set the speed limit, tax your income and run the schools, because sovereignty means the power to decide, in the last resort, on any question at all. The European Union is not like that, and the difference is the single most important thing to grasp about how it works. The Union has no powers of its own. It cannot simply decide that a subject interests it and start passing laws. Every power it holds was handed to it by its members, written down in the Treaties, and it may act only within those limits. Whatever the states did not hand over, they kept. The Union, in other words, does not own its authority. It holds it on loan. This chapter is about that loan: the rule that grants it, the three different kinds of power it produces, and the two questions the Union must answer before it is allowed to use any of them. The rule that grants everything: conferral The founding rule is called the principle of conferral, and it lives in Article 5 of the Treaty on European Union. It says that the Union shall act only within the limits of the competences con‐ ferred upon it by the Member States in the Treaties, and that competences not conferred on the Union remain with the Mem‐ ber States. POWERS ON LOAN Read plainly, this is a constitutional leash. It means the Union can never grant itself a new power; it must always be able to point to a specific article of the Treaties, a legal basis, that authorises what it is about to do. No legal basis, no law. A national parliament that wants to regulate something new simply does so. The Union that wants to regulate something new must first prove it was allowed to. That single asymmetry, easy to forget, is the difference between a sovereign and a delegate, and it shapes everything that follows. The three drawers of power The powers the Member States have lent are not all of the same strength. The Treaties sort them into three drawers, and the exam wants you to know which power sits in which. The first drawer is exclusive competence, set out in Article 3 TFEU. Here, and only here, only the Union may legislate; the Member States are locked out, unless the Union expressly empowers them to act. The list is short and closed, and it is worth memorising because the traps love it: the customs union; the com‐ petition rules needed to make the internal market work; monetary policy for the countries whose currency is the euro; the conserva‐ tion of marine biological resources under the common fisheries policy; and the common commercial policy, meaning external trade. Notice what these have in common. Each only works if there is a single rule. A customs union in which twenty-seven countries set their own tariffs is not a customs union at all. The second drawer, and by far the largest, is shared compet‐ ence, in Article 4 TFEU. Here both the Union and the Member States may act, with one crucial catch: once the Union has legislated in a field, the states may no longer pass laws that cut across it. The field, as lawyers say, is occupied. This is the Union\'s main workshop, and it holds most of what you think of as European policy: the internal market, the environment, agricul‐ ture, consumer protection, transport, energy, and the whole area of freedom, security and justice. When in doubt about where a competence falls, shared is the safe first guess, because it is the default. The third drawer is the weakest, supporting, coordinating or supplementing competence, in Article 6 TFEU. Here the Union may only help. It can encourage, coordinate and top up what the states do, but it may not harmonise their national laws and it may never take the subject over. The states keep the steering wheel; the Union can only offer a push. This is where you find human health, culture, education, tourism, industry, sport and civil protection. If a question tells you the Union has passed a law harmonising one of these, be suspicious: in a supporting field, it is not allowed to. POWERS ON LOAN Only the Union may act Both act; states where the EU has not EU only supports; no harmonising · Freedom, security & justice · Civil protection ALLOWED TO ACT, AND HOW STRONGLY The two questions before acting Here is the subtlety that catches people out. Being allowed to act is not the same as being right to act. Even where a competence exists, the Union must still clear two further tests before it may use it, and both of them also live in Article 5 TEU. The first is subsidiarity. In any area that is not exclusively the Union\'s, it may act only if the objective cannot be sufficiently achieved by the Member States on their own, and can be better achieved at Union level because of the scale or effects of the action. The idea behind it is that decisions should be taken as close to the citizen as possible, and lifted up to the European level only when there is a genuine reason. Note the boundary carefully, because the exam tests it: subsidiarity applies only to non-exclus‐ ive competences. In an exclusive field the question never arises, because the states cannot act there anyway. The second is proportionality. Even when the Union is right to act, the content and form of its action must not go beyond what is necessary to achieve the Treaties\' objectives. If a light- touch measure will do, a heavy one is unlawful. Unlike subsidiarity, proportionality applies to all competences, exclusive ones included. Keep the two apart with a single question each. Subsidiarity asks: should the Union act at all, rather than the states? Proportion‐ ality asks: having decided to act, how far should it go? The first is about who; the second is about how much. rather than the states? FIGURE 2. THE TWO QUESTIONS THE UNION MUST ANSWER BEFORE IT ACTS The watchdogs A principle with no enforcer is just a wish, so the Treaties gave subsidiarity a set of guardians: the national parliaments. Under an arrangement often called the Early Warning System, set out in a protocol attached to the Treaties, every draft EU law is sent to the parliaments of all the Member States, and each may object, in a reasoned opinion, that the proposal breaches subsidiarity, that Brussels is doing what the states could do perfectly well themselves. If enough of them object, they can act together. Reach roughly a third of their combined votes and they raise what is nicknamed a POWERS ON LOAN yellow card, obliging the Commission to review the proposal and justify or withdraw it. Cross a higher threshold and the stronger orange card applies. It is one of the very few places in the system where the parliaments of Europe act as a single collective brake, reaching over the heads of their own governments to check the Union directly. It has rarely been used, but its existence is the point: subsidiarity is watched. Why it matters So the question this chapter began with, who is allowed to act?, has a precise and rather beautiful answer. The Union may act only where its members lent it the power, and never anywhere else. That power falls into one of three drawers, exclusive, shared or supporting, each with its own strength. Even then, in the non- exclusive fields it may act only when acting together is genuinely better, and even then only as far as necessary.',
  5: 'CHAPTER 5  The Guardians of the Law The Court of Justice in Luxembourg, the Commission as watchdog, and the quiet mechanism that turned every national judge into an enforcer of European law. A legal order is only as strong as its ability to be enforced. You can write, in a hundred solemn judgments, that European law is supreme and binds everyone, but words on a page do not make a sover‐ eign government obey. And there is a harder problem still. If European law is to mean the same thing in Dublin and in Sofia, who stops the thousands of national judges across twenty-seven different legal traditions from each reading it their own way, until the single rulebook quietly splinters into twenty-seven? The answer to both problems lives in one institution, in Luxembourg, whose entire purpose the Treaty states in a single line: to ensure that the law is observed. This chapter is about that institution, the Court of Justice, and about the machinery, some of it courtroom drama, some of it quiet paperwork, that makes the law of Chapter 3 actually stick. The court, or rather the courts, in Luxembourg First, clear away the confusion that trips up more candidates than almost anything else. The Court of Justice of the European Uni‐ on, the CJEU, sits in Luxembourg. It is not the human-rights court in Strasbourg, which belongs to the Council of Europe, and it is not the world court in The Hague, which belongs to the United Nations. Luxembourg, European Union, the law of the Treaties. Fix that firmly. The CJEU is not one court but two. The senior of them is the Court of Justice itself, made up of one judge from each Member State, twenty-seven in all, assisted by a group of Advocates Gener‐ al. The Advocates General are a feature the exam likes: they are not judges, but they deliver an independent, reasoned opinion on a case before the judges rule, a sort of impartial first draft of the answer. Their opinions are influential but not binding. Below the Court of Justice sits the General Court, the larger workhorse, with two judges per Member State, which handles the great volume of cases brought by companies and individuals, competition fines, state aid, trademarks and the like. The judges are chosen for their independence, appointed by common accord of the Member States\' governments for renew‐ able terms of six years, with the bench partly renewed every three. And their mission, set out in Article 19 of the Treaty on European Union, is that single grand sentence: to ensure that in the interpretation and application of the Treaties, the law is observed. COURT OF JUSTICE OF THE EU Luxembourg FIGURE 1. THE CJEU IS TWO COURTS — AND NEITHER IS THE STRASBOURG HUMAN-RIGHTS COURT The crown jewel: the preliminary ruling The most important thing the Court does is also the least dramatic, and it is the mechanism on which the whole edifice quietly turns. It is called the preliminary ruling, and it lives in Article 267 TFEU. Picture an ordinary case in an ordinary national court, a tax dispute, a customs charge, a consumer complaint, and somewhere inside it a question of European law that the national judge cannot be sure how to answer. What does this EU rule actually mean? Is this EU act even valid? Under Article 267, that national judge can stop the case and ask the Court of Justice. The courts of last in‐ stance, those from which there is no further appeal, must ask; the lower courts may. The Court of Justice then gives its answer, the answer binds, and the case goes back to the national judge to be decided in light of it. Consider how elegant this is. European law is interpreted once, centrally, for everyone, so it cannot drift into twenty-seven meanings. And every national court, from the humblest tribunal upward, becomes a partner in applying it. This is a dialogue, not a chain of command: the Court of Justice interprets the European rule, but it does not decide the national case, that remains the national judge\'s job. And it was through exactly this channel, an obscure customs dispute and an unpaid electricity bill referred up from national courts, that the great judgments of Chapter 3 reached Luxembourg in the first place. Preliminary ruling The Court interprets the EU rule, uniform for all 27; National court the national court then decides the case. Art 267 Infringement A Member State is taken to Court; financial Commission penalties are possible under Art 260. Art 258–260 Annulment An unlawful EU act is reviewed and may be Any applicant annulled: the Court polices the institutions too. Art 263 FIGURE 2. THE MAIN ROUTES TO THE COURT OF JUSTICE Dragging a state to court The preliminary ruling keeps the law uniform. A second procedure keeps the Member States in line, and here the Court has a partner: the European Commission, which wears, among its several hats, the title Guardian of the Treaties. When a Member State breaks European law, by failing to transpose a directive on time, say, or keeping a national rule the Treaties forbid, the Commission may act. It investigates, it sends formal warnings, and if the state still will not comply, it brings that state before the Court of Justice in an infringement action under Articles 258 to 260 TFEU. If the Court finds against the state, the state is expected to fall into line. And if it still refuses, even after losing, Article 260 allows the Court to impose financial penalties, a lump sum and a daily fine that runs until the state complies. Pause on that. A sovereign nation can be taken to court and fined by that court for breaking European law, and it pays. There is no clearer illustration of what makes this a genuine legal order. Policing the institutions themselves It would be a strange guardian that disciplined only the states and never its own side. So the Court also polices the European institu‐ tions. Remember from Chapter 4 that the Union may act only within the powers conferred on it. The instrument that enforces that limit is the action for annulment, under Article 263 TFEU, which lets the Court strike down an EU act that is unlawful, on grounds such as lack of competence, breach of an essential pro‐ cedural requirement, infringement of the Treaties, or misuse of powers. The Member States, the Parliament, the Council and the Commission may all bring such an action; individuals and companies may too, though under stricter conditions. There is a mirror image as well, the action for failure to act under Article 265, for when an institution unlawfully sits on its hands. Between them, these actions are how the Court holds the Union itself to the leash of Chapter 4. Making the state pay the citizen One piece completes the circle. Direct effect, from Chapter 3, gave individuals rights under European law. But a right with no remedy is cold comfort. In 1991, in a case called Francovich, the Court supplied the remedy. It held that a Member State can be made to compensate individuals for the harm its breach of European law has caused them, for instance where a government\'s failure to transpose a directive on time leaves a worker without protection they should have had. So the citizen who suffers because their own government flouted European law is not left stranded. They can claim damages from that government, in their own national court. Van Gend gave the citizen a right; Costa made that right supreme; Francovich made it worth something. Why it matters The enforcement machine, then, has three targets and one purpose.',
  6: 'CHAPTER 6  Chapter Six, The European Commission — the engine room of the Union. The Engine Room The Commission that proposes almost every European law, guards the Treaties and runs the Union day to day, and why its members answer to Europe, not to their own countries. THE ENGINE ROOM T here is a familiar cartoon of the European Union: a grey warren of offices in Brussels, staffed by faceless bureaucrats inventing rules about the curve of cucumbers. It is a caricature, and like most caricatures it hides the truth by mocking it. Because the institution the cartoon is aimed at is, in fact, the single most important motor in the whole machine. In most areas of European life, not one law can even be discussed by anyone until this institution has proposed it. Nothing moves until it turns the key. That institution is the European Commission, and it is best pictured not as a warren of offices but as the engine room of a great ship: unseen, below decks, unglamorous, and the reason the vessel goes anywhere at all. This chapter opens the part of the book about the Union\'s institutions, and it starts here, in the engine room, because this is where European law begins. What it is, and who sits in it The Commission is the Union\'s executive, its government-in- miniature, and it sits in Brussels. Its leadership is a body called the College of Commissioners: at present one Commissioner from each Member State, twenty-seven in all, each holding a portfolio, a brief for a particular area, much as a national minister holds a department. Over them presides the President of the Commission, and the whole College serves a five-year term, timed to run with the European Parliament. But the College is only the political top of a much larger body, a permanent civil service of thousands, organised into departments called Directorates-General. When people say "the Commission proposed" or "the Commission decided," they usually mean the College; when they say they work for the Commission, they usually mean the machinery beneath it. It is that machinery, incidentally, that a successful EPSO candidate is preparing to join. The oath that defines everything Here is the single most counter-intuitive fact in this chapter, and the one the exam returns to again and again. The German Commissioner is not Germany\'s Commissioner. The French Commissioner does not sit in Brussels to fight France\'s corner. Every Commissioner swears to act in the general interest of the Union, in complete independence, and to take instructions from no government whatsoever, not even the one that nomin‐ ated them. They are not their countries\' delegates or ambassadors; they are, in effect, Europe\'s ministers, sworn to think only of Europe as a whole. Hold on to this, because it is exactly what separates the Commission from the Council you will meet in the next chapter, where ministers really do represent their nations. In the Commission, the flag on your passport is something you are asked to set aside. THE ENGINE ROOM The German Commissioner is not Germany\'s Commissioner. In the Commission, the flag on your passport is something you are asked to set aside. How it is chosen, and how it can be sacked If the Commission has such power, who gives it its authority, and who can take it away? The answer runs, satisfyingly, through the ballot box. The chain begins after each European election. The European Council, the Union\'s presidents and prime ministers, proposes a candidate for President of the Commission, and it must do so tak‐ ing account of the election results, so that the Parliament\'s voters shape the choice. That candidate must then be elected by the European Parliament, by a majority of its members. Next, the President-elect and the Council together assemble the rest of the College from the Member States\' nominees, and each nominee is subjected to a searching public hearing before the relevant parlia‐ mentary committee, where a weak performance can sink a candidacy. Only when the Parliament gives its vote of consent to the College as a whole is the Commission finally appointed by the European Council. proposes a President elects the President parliamentary hearings → appointed (after the EP election) (majority of members) (with the Council) (European Council) And the Parliament keeps a weapon in reserve. It can dismiss the entire College at a stroke through a motion of censure, carried by a two-thirds majority of the votes cast that must also represent a majority of all Members. It has never been pushed all the way through, but in 1999 it came close enough: the Commission led by Jacques Santer, facing a censure it was going to lose over allegations of fraud and mismanagement, resigned as a body rather than be thrown out. The lesson stuck. The engine is powerful, but it answers to the elected Parliament, and it knows it. What it actually does Strip away the detail and the Commission has four great jobs. Learn them as a set, because between them they explain why it mat‐ ters. The first, and the source of nearly all its power, is the right of initiative. In most fields, a European law can only come into being THE ENGINE ROOM if the Commission proposes it first. Parliament and Council may amend and adopt, but they cannot, as a rule, legislate on their own in the ordinary way; the first move is the Commission\'s. Whoever writes the first draft shapes everything that follows, and in Europe that is almost always the Commission. This near-monopoly of ini‐ tiative is what makes it the engine rather than merely a manager. The second is the role you already met in Chapter 5: the Commission is the Guardian of the Treaties. It watches that European law is actually applied, and where a Member State falls short, it is the Commission that investigates and, if need be, hauls the state before the Court of Justice. The third is execution. The Commission runs the Union day to day: it manages the common policies, it implements and oversees the budget, and it adopts the detailed measures that put broad laws into practice. The fourth is to speak for the Union in the wider world. Outside the field of foreign and security policy, it is the Commission that represents the Union externally and, crucially, that negotiates trade and international agreements on behalf of all twenty-seven at once, a formidable power when the counterpart across the table faces the combined weight of the European market. Proposes the laws — a near-monopoly. Ensures EU law is applied; Whoever drafts first shapes everything. brings infringement actions. Runs the common policies day to day; Represents the Union abroad; implements the Union\'s budget. negotiates trade (not foreign policy). Why it matters Put the four jobs together and the picture is complete. The Com‐ mission proposes almost every European law, guards them all once made, runs the machine that applies them, and negotiates for the whole Union abroad, all of it done by people sworn to serve Europe above their own flags, and all of it under the eye of a Parliament that can send the entire College home. That is the engine room. It is not where European law is finally passed, that comes next, and it takes two more institutions to agree. But it is where European law is begun, and understanding that is understanding the first and most decisive move in almost everything the Union does. THE ENGINE ROOM The Spine of the Story The Union\'s executive, seated in Brussels.',
  7: 'CHAPTER 7  The Voice of the Citizens The only institution Europe elects, the three cities it calls home, and its long climb from a powerless assembly to the equal of the Council in making the law. THE VOICE OF THE CITIZENS O f all the institutions in this book, this is the only one you vote for. The Commission you do not elect. The Court you do not elect. The Council is filled by ministers your national election happens to have produced. But once every five years, hundreds of millions of Europeans walk into a polling booth and cast a ballot for this one institution directly, and it is, despite that, the institution they understand least. It is the European Parliament, and its story is the most surpris‐ ing in the whole cast, because it is a story of rising. The Parliament began life as a powerless talking shop of appointed delegates, a body that could debate all it liked and decide nothing at all, and it climbed, election by election and treaty by treaty, until it became the co-equal maker of European law and the chamber that can hire and fire the Commission. This chapter is the story of that climb, and of what the Parliament does now that it has arrived. The house the citizens elect The Parliament\'s defining feature is the one already named: it is the only EU institution elected directly by the citizens. Its mem‐ bers, the MEPs, are chosen every five years by universal suffrage across the whole Union, and their job, in the words of the Treaty, is to represent not the states but the Union\'s citizens. How many are there? The Treaties set a ceiling of 750 members plus the President, 751 seats in all, and at present the Parliament has 720. The seats are shared among the countries by a subtle rule called degressive proportionality, and it is worth understanding because it is a very European compromise. Bigger countries get more seats, as fairness to voters demands, but smaller countries get more seats per head, so that a tiny state is not simply drowned out. No country may have fewer than six MEPs or more than ninety- six, a cap that the largest, Germany, reaches. And here is a detail that echoes the Commission from the last chapter, with a twist. MEPs do not sit by nationality. They sit by political group, gathering across borders by ideology, so that a German socialist and a Portuguese socialist share a bench and argue across the aisle with a German and a Portuguese conservative who share the bench opposite. In the Parliament, it is politics, not passports, that seats you. The parliament with three homes Now the single oddest fact about the Parliament, and one examiners adore. It does not have one home. It has three, in three different countries. Its official seat is Strasbourg, in France, where the main plen‐ ary sessions, the great set-piece votes of the full house, are held twelve times a year. Much of the detailed work, the committee meetings and additional sittings, happens in Brussels, alongside the other institutions. And the Parliament\'s administration, its Gener‐ al Secretariat, sits in Luxembourg. Once a month the entire oper‐ ation, people, papers and all, decamps from Brussels to Strasbourg for the plenary and then troops back again, a costly, much-mocked, THE VOICE OF THE CITIZENS treaty-mandated migration. If you remember one thing, remember this: the official seat is Strasbourg. Do not confuse that Stras‐ bourg with the human-rights court from Chapter 5, which sits in the same city but belongs to an entirely different organisation. 12 plenary sessions a year additional sittings administration The long climb To understand why the Parliament matters, you have to see how little it once did. It began, back in the coal and steel days, as the Common Assembly, and its members were not elected at all; they were national politicians sent over from their home parliaments to sit part-time in Europe. It could hold debates and pass opinions that everyone was free to ignore. It was, in a real sense, an orna‐ ment. Two changes transformed it. The first came in 1979, with the first direct elections. Once the citizens themselves were choosing it, the Parliament had something no appointed body has: a democratic mandate, and a mandate is a lever for demanding power. The second change was slower, a long series of treaties, the Single European Act, Maastricht, Amsterdam and, decisively, Lis‐ bon, each of which handed the Parliament more authority, until it stood level with the Council as a maker of most European law. In a single generation it travelled the whole distance from talking shop to legislature. Very few parliaments in history have grown up so fast. What it does now The grown-up Parliament has three great powers. Learn them as a trio. The first is that it makes the law, jointly with the Council. Under what is called the ordinary legislative procedure, the co- decision that Lisbon turned into the normal way of doing things, a proposal from the Commission becomes law only if both the Parliament and the Council approve it. The Parliament cannot start a law, remember, that is the Commission\'s right of initiative, but nothing can pass over the Parliament\'s objection. It is one of the Union\'s two co-legislators, and that single fact is the measure of how far it has climbed. The second is that it holds the purse. Together with the Coun‐ cil, the Parliament is the Union\'s budgetary authority: it adopts the annual budget and must give its consent to the long-term spending plans. European money does not flow without the elected chamber\'s say-so. The third is that it controls the executive. It is the Parliament that elects the Commission\'s President, that grills and consents to the College, that questions Commissioners and mounts commit‐ THE VOICE OF THE CITIZENS tees of inquiry, and that, as Chapter 6 showed, can dismiss the entire Commission through a motion of censure. The chamber the citizens elect is the chamber that watches the engine room. The triangle at last With this chapter the machinery of law-making finally comes into focus, and it is worth pausing to see the whole shape. The Com‐ mission proposes a law. The Parliament and the Council co-de‐ cide it. A proposal, by itself, is nothing; it becomes European law only when the citizens\' chamber, this one, and the states\' cham‐ ber, the Council of the next chapter, have both said yes. That triangle, one institution to propose and two to agree, is how nearly all of Europe\'s laws are made. THE COMMISSION proposes both must agree the citizens\' chamber (elected) the states\' chamber (ministers) EUROPEAN LAW ADOPTED CIDE Why it matters So the European Parliament is the democratic heart of the Union: the one institution its people choose, risen within living memory from a powerless assembly to the co-maker of the Union\'s laws, the holder of its purse, and the watchdog of its executive. It is easy to overlook and easy to caricature, the distant chamber in a city most voters will never visit. But it is, quietly and genuinely, the great democratic achievement of the entire European project, and it is only half of the story of how Europe legislates. The other half is where the nations themselves sit down at the table, and that is the Council. THE VOICE OF THE CITIZENS The Spine of the Story The only directly elected EU institution; it represents the cit‐ izens (Art 14 TEU). Members: MEPs, elected every five years by universal suffrage.',
  8: 'CHAPTER 8  The Table of Nations The summit of leaders who steer the Union, the ministers who make its laws, and the older, unrelated body that only shares their name. H ere is the single most confusing thing in all of European knowledge, and the one that quietly costs candidates marks every single year. There are three different bodies with the word Council in their name. Two of them belong to the European Union and do entirely different jobs; the third does not belong to the Union at all. Learn to tell them apart, cleanly and instantly, and you have won some of the easiest and most reliable marks in the whole exam. Muddle them, as thousands do, and you will throw those marks away. This chapter untangles the knot, and it does so around a single image. In the last two chapters you met institutions where people are asked to forget their nationality: the Commissioners sworn to serve Europe, the MEPs seated by party rather than passport. The Council is the opposite. It is the table where the nations sit as nations, to defend and reconcile their national interests. The European Council: the leaders who steer The first of the Union\'s two Councils is the summit of the people at the very top. The European Council is made up of the Heads of State or Government of the twenty-seven, the presidents and prime ministers, meeting together, usually four times a year in Brussels, alongside its own permanent President and the President of the Commission. Its job is to set the Union\'s general political direction and pri‐ orities: the great strategic choices, the responses to crises, the decisions to open the door to a new member, the impetus behind the next treaty. It is the bridge of the ship, where the course is set. But now the limit that the exam loves above all others: the European Council does not legislate. It points the Union where to go; it does not write the laws that get it there. Its President, a figure created by the Treaty of Lisbon, is elected by the European Council itself for two and a half years, renewable once, and chairs the summits and represents the Union at the level of leaders. The European Council works, as a rule, by consensus. The Council of the European Union: the ministers who legislate The Union\'s second Council could hardly be more different, though its name is only one word away. The Council of the European Union, often called simply the Council or the Council of Ministers, is made up not of leaders but of government minis‐ ters, one from each Member State. But here is its peculiarity: which minister attends depends entirely on the subject. When the topic is farming, the agriculture ministers gather; when it is money, the finance ministers, in the configuration known as ECOFIN; when it is foreign affairs, the foreign ministers. These are its config‐ urations, one legal body with a constantly changing cast. And this is the Council that actually makes law. It is the co-le‐ gislator alongside the Parliament, the states\' half of the triangle you met in Chapter 7, and together with the Parliament it holds the budget. Two features of it are heavily tested. First, its presidency rotates: every six months a different Member State takes its turn chairing the Council\'s work, so that each country, large or small, gets its moment at the helm. The one exception is the Foreign Af‐ fairs Council, chaired permanently by the High Representative for foreign affairs. Second, it usually decides by qualified majority voting, and the modern rule is a clever double majority: to pass, a measure needs the support of 55% of the Member States (at least fifteen of them) representing at least 65% of the Union\'s popula‐ tion. Both thresholds must be met at once, so that neither a bloc of small states nor a huddle of giants can decide alone. For the most sensitive matters, taxation, foreign policy, treaty change, the bar is higher still: unanimity. The European Council is the leaders, and it steers. The Coun‐ cil of the EU is the ministers, and it legislates. Bosses point; ministers pass laws. Telling the two EU Councils apart Since this is where the marks are won and lost, fix the contrast in a single breath. The European Council is the leaders; it steers and does not legislate; and it has a stable, elected President. The Council of the EU is the ministers; it legislates; and its presidency rotates every six months. One sets the direction, the other writes the law. Keep those two sentences and you will never confuse them again. heads of state / government one per state, by configuration Strasbourg · 46 states Sets political direction Co-legislator (with EP) Human rights (ECHR) Human Rights President elected 2.5 yrs every six months separate from the EU FIGURE 1. THREE "COUNCILS" — TWO ARE EU INSTITU‐ TIONS, ONE IS NOT The imposter at the table: the Council of Europe Now the third body, and the deepest trap of all, because it is not part of the European Union in any way. The Council of Europe is an entirely separate and older organisation. It was founded in 1949, it is based in Strasbourg, and it has forty-six member states, far more than the Union\'s twenty-seven, stretching across almost the whole continent. Its purpose has nothing to do with the single market or with EU legislation. Its great achievement is the European Convention on Human Rights, and the court that enforces it, the European Court of Human Rights, the Strasbourg court you met back in Chapter 5. The Council of Europe passes no EU laws, sits in no EU triangle, and answers to none of the Union\'s rules. If ever an exam question slips it into a list of the European Union\'s institutions, that answer is wrong. Remember it as the odd one out: older, larger, human rights, Strasbourg, and not the EU. QUALIFIED MAJORITY = DOUBLE MAJORITY FIGURE 2. QUALIFIED MAJORIT Y VOTING — A MEASURE Why it matters So the knot untangles into three clean strands. The European Council: the leaders, who steer and do not legislate. The Council of the European Union: the ministers, who legislate and whose presidency rotates. And the Council of Europe: not the Union at all, but a separate human-rights body in Strasbourg, born in 1949. Two tables inside the Union, and one outside it. And with the ministers\' Council now in its place, the whole machine of European law-making finally stands complete. The Commission proposes. The Parliament and this Council decide. The engine, the citizens\' chamber and the states\' table, three institutions and one triangle, are all now on the page. The nations have taken their seats. The Spine of the Story THE EUROPEAN COUNCIL · ART 15 TEU The Heads of State or Government + its President + the President of the Commission. Sets the Union\'s general political direction and priorities. Does not legislate. Meets ~4 times a year in Brussels; works by consensus. Its President is elected for 2.5 years (renewable once). THE COUNCIL OF THE EU · ART 16 TEU The "Council of Ministers": one minister per state, varying by configuration (ECOFIN, Foreign Affairs, Agriculture...). The co-legislator with the Parliament and joint budgetary authority. Presidency rotates every six months (except For‐ eign Affairs, chaired by the High Representative). Usually de‐ cides by qualified majority = 55% of states (≥15) + 65% of population; unanimity for sensitive areas. Prepared by COREPER. THE COUNCIL OF EUROPE · NOT THE EU A separate, older organisation.',
  9: 'CHAPTER 9  The Guardians of the Money The bank in Frankfurt that protects what the euro is worth, and the court in Luxembourg that checks how every euro is spent. € THE GUARDIANS OF THE MONEY E very institution so far has been about power and law: who proposes, who decides, who judges. But the Union also runs money, on a scale that is hard to picture. It manages a shared currency in the pockets of more than three hundred million people, and it spends a budget that runs past a trillion euros across each seven-year cycle. Money on that scale needs guarding, and it needs guarding in two different senses. Someone must protect what the money is worth, and someone else must check that it is spent honestly. Two institutions do exactly that. They are the quietest members of the whole cast, they sit not in Brussels but in Frankfurt and Luxembourg, and they are both fiercely independent. And one of them, despite its name, is not at all the kind of thing you would expect. These are the guardians of the money. The bank that guards the euro\'s worth The first guardian is the European Central Bank, the ECB, and it lives in Frankfurt, in Germany. Fix that seat in your memory now, because it is a favourite of the exam: not Brussels, not Strasbourg, not Luxembourg, but Frankfurt. The ECB is the guardian of the euro. Its task is to run the mon‐ etary policy of the euro area, the twenty countries that share the currency, and its overriding mission, written into the Treaty, is price stability: keeping inflation low and steady, close to two per cent, so that the money in your pocket holds its value from one year to the next. It pursues that mission chiefly by setting the key interest rates for the euro area, the levers that make borrowing cheaper or dearer across the whole currency zone, and it alone holds the sole right to authorise the issue of euro banknotes. No one else may print the money. Why the bank answers to no one Here is the single most important fact about the ECB, and the one most likely to be tested. It is fiercely independent. By the Treaty, neither the ECB nor the national central banks may seek or take instructions from any government or any EU institution, and governments are forbidden even to try to influence them. Why build a firewall that high? Because a central bank that politicians could lean on is a dangerous thing. A government facing an election is always tempted to print a little more money to spread a little more cheer, and a currency run for short-term popularity soon loses its value altogether. History is littered with the wreckage. So the euro\'s value is deliberately walled off from politics, placed in the hands of people no minister can command. The same logic shapes the top job: the President of the ECB is appointed for a single, long term of eight years that cannot be renewed, long enough to outlast the political cycle, and non-renewable so that the holder never has to please anyone in order to keep the post. Independence is not a detail of the ECB. It is the whole point of it. THE GUARDIANS OF THE MONEY A currency run for short-term popularity soon loses its value altogether. Independence is not a detail of the ECB; it is the whole point of it. The court that guards the money\'s use, and is not a court The second guardian sits in Luxembourg, and it is called the European Court of Auditors. But here is the twist the exam adores, so read it twice. Despite the word "Court" in its name, it is not a court in any judicial sense at all. It cannot try anyone, it cannot fine anyone, it cannot send anyone to prison. It hands down no judgments. What it is, is the Union\'s independent external auditor, its financial conscience. Its job is to check that the Union\'s money is raised and spent properly: lawfully, correctly, and with genuine value for money, so that the trillion-euro budget is not quietly wasted or misused. It has one member from each Member State, appointed for renewable six-year terms and sworn to act in complete independence. Each year it examines the Union\'s accounts and publishes a report, at the heart of which is a formal statement of assurance, a verdict on whether the books are reliable and whether the money was spent within the rules. It cannot punish, but it can expose, and exposure has teeth of its own. Its findings are the evidence on which the Parliament de‐ cides each year whether to sign off on the Commission\'s handling of the budget, a step known as granting discharge. An auditor with no power to prosecute still shapes how every European euro is watched. Guards what the euro is worth Guards how the money is spent Monetary policy · price stability The Union\'s external auditor Sole right to issue euro notes Audits & reports; cannot punish President: 8 years, non-renewable One member per Member State FIGURE 1. TWO GUARDIANS OF THE MONEY — ONE PRO‐ The seven, complete With these two guardians, the full cast of the Union\'s institutions is finally on the page, and this is the moment to gather them. The Treaty names exactly seven institutions of the European Union, and "which of the following is an institution of the Union" is one of the most dependable questions in the entire exam. They are the European Parliament, the European Council, the Council of the European Union, the European Commission, the Court of THE GUARDIANS OF THE MONEY Justice of the European Union, the European Central Bank, and the Court of Auditors. Seven, no more. The traps are always plausible impostors, bodies that sound official but are not among the seven: the Coun‐ cil of Europe from the last chapter, the European Ombudsman, the advisory committees, the dozens of specialised agencies. When the question asks for an institution, the answer is one of the seven, and now you know them all. THE SEVEN INSTITUTIONS · ARTICLE 13 TEU gold & steel: earlier chapters · ember: this chapter FIGURE 2. ALL SEVEN INSTITUTIONS OF THE UNION — THIS CHAPTER COMPLETES THE SET Why it matters So the money has its guardians, and they guard it in the two ways that matter. The ECB in Frankfurt protects what the euro is worth, sealed off from politics so that no government can quietly debase it. The Court of Auditors in Luxembourg protects how the Union\'s money is spent, exposing waste and error even though it holds no power to punish. Neither makes headlines. Neither is what anyone pictures when they imagine "Brussels." But between them they complete the seven institutions of the Union, and they stand watch over the one thing every citizen, in the end, actually notices: the money. THE GUARDIANS OF THE MONEY The Spine of the Story THE EUROPEAN CENTRAL BANK Seat: Frankfurt. Runs monetary policy for the euro area (the twenty euro countries). Primary objective: price stability. In‐ dependent — takes no instructions. Holds the sole right to authorise euro banknotes; sets the key interest rates. Main decision-maker: the Governing Council; run day to day by the Executive Board. The President serves a single eight-year, non-renewable term. THE EUROPEAN COURT OF AUDITORS Seat: Luxembourg. The Union\'s independent external aud‐ itor: checks that EU money is raised and spent lawfully, cor‐ rectly and with value for money. Not a judicial court — it cannot punish or prosecute. One member per Member State, six-year renewable terms. Publishes an annual report and a statement of assurance, feeding the Parliament\'s dis‐ charge decision.',
  10: 'CHAPTER 10  The Four Freedoms How twenty-seven national economies were fused into a single market, and the four kinds of freedom that hold it together. F or nine chapters this book has been about power: who makes the law, who guards it, who spends the money. Now we turn to what the Union actually does with all that machinery, and the answer, above everything else, is that it runs a market. Not a market in the small sense of a place to buy and sell, but a single economic space of some 450 million people in which goods, workers, services and money move as freely across national borders as they do within a single country. This is the most tangible thing the Union has ever built. It is the reason a shopper in Lisbon can buy German tools and Dutch cheese without a tariff, the reason a Spanish lawyer can move to Luxembourg to practise, the reason a bank in one country can lend in another. It is the beating heart of the whole project, and it stands on four great pillars that the treaties call the four freedoms. One market from twenty-seven The formal name is the internal market, or the single market, and the Treaty defines it in a single elegant phrase: an area without in‐ ternal frontiers in which the free movement of goods, persons, services and capital is ensured. Four freedoms, one market. The ambition, first sketched at Rome in 1957 and driven to completion by the great 1992 programme of the Single European Act, was to rub out the economic borders between the members and fuse twenty-seven national economies into one. Beneath all four freedoms runs a single principle, and it is worth naming because it explains almost every rule that follows: no dis‐ THE FOUR FREEDOMS crimination on grounds of nationality. A French worker in Ger‐ many, a Polish product in France, an Italian bank in Spain, none may be treated worse simply for being foreign. Strip the single market down to one idea, and that is it. The first freedom: goods The free movement of goods rests on two achievements, one easy to see and one subtle. The visible one is the customs union: no customs duties at all between the members, and a common external tariff facing the rest of the world, so that once a product is inside the Union it can travel to any corner of it without paying a penny more at any border. The subtle achievement was tearing down the hidden barriers, the national product rules that quietly kept foreign goods out without ever mentioning a tariff. Here the Court of Justice worked its familiar magic, in a 1979 case called Cassis de Dijon. Germany had tried to block a French blackcurrant liqueur on the ground that it was too weak to meet the German definition of a "liqueur." The Court refused to allow it, and in doing so laid down one of the most important principles in the whole single market: mutual re‐ cognition. A product lawfully made and sold in one Member State may, in principle, be sold in all the others. A country can no longer use its own standards as a disguised wall. Genuine exceptions survive, a state may still block goods to protect health, safety or public morality, but these are narrow and may never be a mask for protectionism. Customs union — no internal tariffs Workers move freely (Art 45) Mutual recognition (Cassis de Dijon) Establishment & citizenship Provided across borders (Art 56) Money & investment move freely (Art 63) an architect, an insurer, a consultant even with third countries SINGLE MARKET The second freedom: persons The second freedom is the one people feel in their own lives. Under the free movement of persons, a worker from any Member State may go to any other to take a job, on equal terms with the locals, with no discrimination in hiring, pay or working conditions. Businesses and the self-employed enjoy a parallel right, the freedom of establishment, to set up and run a firm anywhere in the Union. And above them both sits citizenship of the Union, the Maastricht creation from Chapter 2, which gives every citizen the right to move and reside freely across the whole territory. This is the freedom that turns the map into a lived reality: the Spanish lawyer in Luxembourg, the Polish nurse in Ireland, the German engineer in Portugal. It is worth keeping it distinct from Schengen, the abolition of passport checks you met in Chapter 1; the two are related but not the same. Schengen removes the border post; free movement of persons gives you the right to work and live on the other side of it. THE FOUR FREEDOMS The third and fourth freedoms: services and capital The remaining two freedoms are less visible but just as vital. The free movement of services lets a business in one country provide its services in another, an architect designing a building across the border, an insurer covering clients Union-wide, a consultant advising a foreign client, without having to clear a fresh set of national hurdles each time. And the free movement of capital lets money itself move, in‐ vestments, loans, the purchase of property and shares, flowing across borders without obstruction. This is the freedom that makes cross-border banking and investment possible and that underpins the euro. It carries one feature the others lack: capital moves freely not only within the Union but, to a significant degree, between the Union and the wider world. It is the most outward-facing of the four. SINGLE MARKET ◀ the EU + free movement of goods, persons, services, capital DEEPER INTEGRATION CUSTOMS UNION + a common external tariff FREE TRADE AREA no tariffs between members GOES FURTHER THAN A CUSTOMS UNION The freedoms are not absolute One caution completes the picture, and the exam likes to test it. None of the four freedoms is unconditional. A Member State may still restrict them, but only for genuine reasons of public policy, public security or public health, and only within tight limits, and never as disguised protectionism. The rule to hold in your head is this: the default is freedom, and the restrictions are the narrow, justified exception, not the other way round. Why it matters So the single market is the Union\'s beating heart, and the four freedoms, goods, persons, services and capital, are its four chambers. Between them they took a continent of walled-off national economies and fused them into the largest single market on Earth. Every other economic policy in this part of the book, competition, the budget, trade, exists to serve or to protect this one creation. And more than any treaty article or institution, it is the four freedoms that make the word "European" something a citizen can actually live, rather than merely read. THE FOUR FREEDOMS The Spine of the Story TFEU An area without internal frontiers with the free movement of goods, persons, services and capital. A shared compet‐ ence. Underlying rule: no discrimination on grounds of na‐ tionality. Sketched at Rome (1957), completed by the 1992 programme (Single European Act). THE FOUR FREEDOMS FREE‐ KEY POINTS DOM Goods Customs union (no internal tariffs + common external tariff); mutual recognition (Cassis de Dijon, 1979) Persons Workers (Art 45), establishment (Art 49), cit‐ izenship (Art 21) Services Provided across borders (Art 56) Capital Money & investment (Art 63); also, to a degree, with third countries Limits: restrictions only for public policy, security or health, narrowly, and never as disguised protectionism. THE FOUR FREEDOMS ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. The four freedoms: goods, persons, services, capital. There is no fifth. 2.',
  11: 'CHAPTER 11  The Referee Competition policy, and how the Union keeps its single market fair — against cartels, monopolies, mergers, and even governments bearing gifts. THE REFEREE T he last chapter opened the market; it did not make it fair. An open market and a fair one are not the same thing, and left entirely to itself an open market does not stay open for long. The powerful rig it. Big firms quietly agree to fix their prices instead of competing. A dominant company crushes the newcomer before it can grow. Two giants merge until only one is left. And, most tempting of all, governments reach into the treasury to bankroll their own national champions, rebuilding with cash the very barriers the four freedoms tore down. So the Union appointed a referee, and gave it real teeth. This is competition policy, one of the oldest and most muscular of all the Union\'s powers, enforced by a Commission that can fine the largest companies on Earth billions of euros and can forbid a sovereign government from handing money to its own industry. Its job is to keep the level playing field genuinely level, so that the single market delivers what it promises: lower prices, more choice and real competition for the citizen. The threats to a fair market There are four ways a market can be rigged, and competition policy has a weapon against each. Firms can collude, agreeing secretly not to compete. A single firm can abuse its dominance to shut others out. Companies can merge until competition simply disappears. And governments can subsidise, tilting the field with public money. The Treaty tackles all four. Cartels and abuse: the antitrust rules The first two threats are handled by what the world calls antitrust, set out in two famous articles. Article 101 TFEU prohibits agree‐ ments between firms that restrict competition: the price-fixing rings, the market-sharing pacts, the secret cartels in which sup‐ posed rivals conspire to rob their customers. Such agreements are automatically void, and the firms behind them can be fined enormous sums. Article 102 TFEU tackles the lone giant. It prohibits the abuse of a dominant position. Read that carefully, because it hides the classic trap: it is not illegal to be dominant. Growing large by be‐ ing better than your rivals is exactly what the market rewards. What the Treaty forbids is abusing that dominance, using overwhelming market power to bully suppliers, to impose unfair terms, or to price rivals out of existence. Dominance is allowed; the abuse of it is not. Price-fixing & market-sharing Dominance is legal; Big mergers must be notified; Subsidies that distort are barred; clear, condition or block the Commission must approve THE REFEREE Mergers and the €10 billion whistle The third threat, mergers, is caught by merger control. When two large companies with a Union-wide reach wish to combine, they cannot simply do it; they must first notify the Commission, which examines whether the merger would harm competition and can clear it, attach conditions, or block it outright. A deal that would leave consumers at the mercy of a new monopoly does not go ahead. Behind all of this stands the enforcer. The European Commis‐ sion is the guardian of competition, and its powers are formidable: it can launch investigations, carry out surprise inspections, and impose fines of up to ten per cent of a company\'s total worldwide turnover for the gravest breaches, sums that reach into the billions and that even the world\'s largest corporations cannot shrug off. Competition rules for the internal market are, you will recall from Chapter 4, one of the Union\'s exclusive competences, which is why it is Brussels, not the national capitals, that blows this particular whistle. The distinctively European rule: state aid Now the fourth threat, and the one that makes European competi‐ tion policy genuinely unusual in the world. In most countries, a government is perfectly free to hand money to its favourite companies. In the European Union, it is not. This is the control of state aid, under Articles 107 to 109 TFEU, and it is the rule most likely to surprise you. The logic is simple once you see it. If France could pour subsidies into French firms and Germany into German ones, the single market would collapse back into a contest of national treasuries, and the four freedoms would mean nothing. So, in prin‐ ciple, state aid that distorts competition and affects trade between Member States is prohibited, and a government that wishes to grant it must, as a rule, obtain the Commission\'s ap‐ proval first. The Commission can allow aid that serves a genuine common purpose, supporting a poor region, funding research, meeting a crisis, but it can also order a company to pay back aid that was granted unlawfully. No other major economy polices its own governments\' cheque-books quite like this, and it exists for one reason: to stop states from quietly cheating the single market. (or order it repaid) Unique to the EU: even a government cannot simply subsidise its own firms. Why it matters So the referee patrols four fronts: cartels (Article 101), abuse of dominance (Article 102), mergers, and state aid (Articles 107 to 109). Against all four it wields the same purpose, to keep the THE REFEREE market genuinely competitive, and the same enforcer, a Commis‐ sion armed with billion-euro fines. Competition policy is not a footnote to the single market; it is its bodyguard. The four freedoms build the field, and the referee makes sure the game played on it is fair. The Spine of the Story WHY COMPETITION POLICY An open market is not automatically a fair one. Competition policy keeps it competitive, for the benefit of consumers. Competition rules for the internal market are an exclusive competence; the Commission is the enforcer. THE REFEREE THE FOUR FRONTS Cartels / Art 101 Bans agreements that restrict al) clear, condition or block State aid Art 107–109 Distorting subsidies prohib‐ ited; Commission approves Enforcer: the Commission; fines up to 10% of worldwide turnover; can order unlawful aid repaid. ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Being dominant is not illegal; abusing dominance is (Art 102). 2. Article 101 = cartels/agreements; Article 102 = abuse of dominance. Do not swap them. 3. State aid is, in principle, prohibited and needs the Com‐ mission\'s approval. 4. The Commission is the enforcer, with fines up to 10% of global turnover. 5. Competition rules for the internal market are an exclusive competence. 6. Merger control: large mergers must be notified and cleared first.',
  12: 'CHAPTER 12  One Money, Many Masters The euro and Economic and Monetary Union — who runs the single currency, who is allowed to join it, and the fault line running through its heart. O f all the things the Union has ever asked of its members, the boldest was this: give up your money. Not a tariff, not a rule, but the franc, the mark, the lira, the peseta, the coins with a nation\'s kings and heroes on them, surrendered for a shared currency with no country\'s face on it at all. That so many proud old nations agreed is astonishing. That the result has largely held is more astonishing still, because a single currency is one of the strangest and most delicate machines in the whole of European construction. Here is why it is strange. A shared currency means one interest rate for twenty-one very different economies, from Germany to Greece, set by a single bank. And it means a currency shared by twenty-one governments who nonetheless still write their own budgets and set their own taxes. One money, many masters. This chapter is about that machine, Economic and Monetary Union, how it works, who is allowed into it, and the fault line running through its heart. What EMU actually is The formal name is Economic and Monetary Union, or EMU, and it has two halves that the exam likes to keep straight. The mon‐ etary half is the single currency itself, run centrally. The economic half is the taxing and spending of the Member States, which is not centralised but merely coordinated. EMU is a union that is com‐ plete on the monetary side and deliberately unfinished on the economic one. ONE MONEY, MANY MASTERS At its centre is the euro, born as an electronic currency in 1999 and arriving as notes and coins in 2002 (the two dates you separ‐ ated back in Chapter 1). Today it is the currency of twenty-one Member States, the newest of them Bulgaria, which adopted the euro on 1 January 2026, a zone usually called the euro area or the eurozone. Not every EU country uses it, a point to hold on to: the euro area is a subset of the Union, not the whole of it. Who runs the money The monetary half has a single master, and you already met it in Chapter 9: the European Central Bank in Frankfurt. For the euro area, monetary policy is one of the Union\'s exclusive competences, so the ECB, and the ECB alone, sets the single interest rate for the whole zone and steers the currency toward its overriding goal of price stability. Twenty national central banks feed into it, but the decisions are made in common, in Frankfurt, walled off from politics. On the monetary side, the union is real and complete. The fault line Now the economic half, and the crack that runs through the entire design. While the euro area shares one monetary policy, it does not share a common budget or a common tax system. Taxing and spending remain national. Each government still decides how much to spend, how much to borrow, and how to tax, answering to its own voters, not to Frankfurt or Brussels. Sit with that for a moment, because it is the single most important idea in the chapter. The euro area runs on one monet‐ ary policy and twenty-one national fiscal policies at once. One money, many masters. It is a marriage of a single shared currency to twenty-one separate national cheque-books, and when those cheque-books pull hard in different directions, as they did in the debt crisis of the early 2010s, the strain falls on the shared currency that cannot simply be devalued by any one member to relieve it. The genius and the fragility of the euro are the same fact. FAULT LINE THE ECB one interest rate · exclusive FIGURE 1. ONE MONEY, MANY MASTERS — EMU\'S CENTRAL ASYMMETRY Who is allowed in: the convergence criteria Because one reckless member can endanger everyone\'s money, a country cannot simply decide to adopt the euro. It must first prove it has converged with the others by meeting the convergence cri‐ teria, sometimes called the Maastricht criteria, a checklist of economic health. The two figures most worth memorising concern public finances: a government\'s annual deficit must not exceed ONE MONEY, MANY MASTERS 3% of GDP, and its total debt must not exceed 60% of GDP (or be falling toward it). Alongside these sit tests on inflation, on the stability of the exchange rate, and on long-term interest rates. Clear the bar, and a country may join; fail it, and it must wait. FIGURE 2. THE CONVERGENCE CRITERIA — A COUNTRY The rules that hold it together The same two numbers do a second job. Once inside, members are bound by the Stability and Growth Pact, reformed in 2024 but still anchored to those same 3% deficit and 60% debt thresholds. The Pact exists to keep national budgets disciplined even after a country has joined, so that no single government can borrow recklessly and drag the shared currency down with it. It is the fiscal fence around the monetary union, an attempt to manage the fault line rather than remove it. A few final pieces complete the map. Some members stay outside the euro by choice, most famously Denmark, which nego‐ tiated a formal opt-out; others remain outside because they have not yet met the criteria, though they are, in principle, committed to joining one day. And the euro-area finance ministers meet informally as the Eurogroup to coordinate the economic half that no institution fully controls. Why it matters So the euro is at once the single market\'s crowning achievement and its boldest gamble: a shared money among nations that still govern their own economies. It works, day to day, because an independent bank guards its value and a set of fiscal rules guards its discipline. And it strains, in every crisis, along the fault line it was born with, one money, many masters. Understanding that single tension is understanding almost everything the news will ever tell you about the euro. ONE MONEY, MANY MASTERS The Spine of the Story Economic and Monetary Union = a monetary union (the single currency, run centrally) + an economic side (national taxing and spending, only coordinated). The euro: electronic 1999, cash 2002; today the currency of twenty-one Member States, Bulgaria the newest since 1 January 2026. The euro area is a subset of the EU. The ECB (Frankfurt) sets the single interest rate and guards price stability; euro-area monetary policy is an exclusive com‐ petence. But fiscal policy stays national (coordinated only). One monetary policy, twenty-one national budgets: one money, many masters. JOINING & THE RULES Convergence criteria: deficit ≤ 3% of GDP, debt ≤ 60% of GDP, plus inflation, exchange-rate stability and long-term interest rates. Stability and Growth Pact: keeps budgets disciplined using the same 3% / 60% values. Denmark has an opt-out; the Eurogroup is the euro-area finance ministers. ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Monetary policy is centralised (the ECB); fiscal policy is national. This asymmetry is the heart of EMU. 2. Euro area ≠ EU: twenty use the euro, twenty-seven are members. 3. Convergence figures: deficit ≤ 3% of GDP, debt ≤ 60% of GDP. 4. The euro: electronic 1999, cash 2002. 5. Denmark has an opt-out; other non-euro states are committed to join. 6. Euro-area monetary policy is exclusive; general economic policy is only coordinated. ONE MONEY, MANY MASTERS',
  13: 'CHAPTER 13  The Money of the Union How the Union raises its money without a tax of its own, plans it seven years at a time, spends it — and why it can never run a deficit. E very government runs on money, and the Union is no exception: it spends well over €170 billion a year to run everything this book has described. But the Union\'s relationship with money is peculiar, and the peculiarity is the whole story of this chapter. A nation raises its money by tax‐ ing its citizens directly. The European Union cannot. There is no European income tax, no cheque you write to Brussels. So where does the money come from, where does it go, and why is the Union, almost alone among the world\'s public authorities, forbidden from ever running a deficit? Where the money comes from The Union funds itself from what the treaties call own resources, and there are, in essence, three of them, plus a newer fourth. The first is the most fitting: the customs duties collected on goods entering the Union from the outside world, the common external tariff of the customs union you met in Chapter 10. Tax the world\'s imports at the Union\'s border, and the proceeds belong to the Union itself. The second is a small slice of each country\'s value- added tax, a modest percentage of a harmonised VAT base. And the third, much the largest, is a contribution from each Member State based on its Gross National Income, its overall economic size, so that richer countries pay more and the whole budget is balanced up to the amount required. To these three a fourth was added in 2021, a contribution based on the weight of non-recycled plastic packaging waste. THE MONEY OF THE UNION Notice what this means. The Union\'s money comes chiefly from national contributions and from customs duties, not from a tax of its own. And because the members are handing over their own resources, the system that governs them is decided by the Council acting unanimously, and must then be ratified by every Member State in the ordinary national way. On the money, the states keep a very tight grip. WHERE THE MONEY COMES FROM · OWN RESOURCES No direct EU tax. Bar widths are illustrative, not to scale. UNION\'S OWN RESOURCES The seven-year plan: the MFF The Union does not budget only year by year. It plans in long blocks, through the Multiannual Financial Framework, the MFF, a spending plan that runs for seven years and fixes the ceil‐ ings, the maximum that may be spent, for each broad category. The MFF gives the Union something governments living election to election rarely have: predictability, a settled horizon for long projects. It is adopted by the Council acting unanimously, with the consent of the European Parliament, so both the states and the citizens\' chamber must agree before the Union\'s finances are set for the better part of a decade. The yearly budget Within those seven-year ceilings sits the annual budget, decided every year. Here the pattern from Chapter 7 returns: the Commis‐ sion draws up the draft, and the European Parliament and the Council together adopt it. The two of them are the Union\'s budgetary authority, the states\' chamber and the citizens\' chamber jointly holding the purse. No euro is committed without their agreement. Where the money goes And where does all of it go? Overwhelmingly, historically, to two great purposes. The first is agriculture, the Common Agricultural Policy, the Union\'s oldest big-spending commitment, which supports Europe\'s farmers and rural areas. The second is cohesion, the policy of channelling money to the Union\'s poorer regions so that they can catch up with the richer ones, building roads, funding training, narrowing the gap between a wealthy German city and a struggling rural region far away. Between them, farming and cohesion have long taken the lion\'s share. The rest funds research and the single market, the Union\'s action in the wider world, the management of borders and migration, and the running of the institutions themselves. THE MONEY OF THE UNION WHERE THE MONEY GOES · MAIN SPENDING Cohesion — poorer regions catch up Agriculture (CAP) — farmers & rural areas The two big blocks — agriculture & cohesion Research & single market External action, borders Administration COHESION LEAD The golden rule: no deficit Now the rule that sets the Union apart from almost every national government on Earth. A nation, short of money, simply borrows the difference and runs a deficit. The European Union may not. Its budget must, by the Treaty, balance every single year: what it plans to spend can never exceed what it expects to raise. It cannot borrow to cover ordinary spending. This is why the fights over the MFF and the annual budget are so fierce, there is no overdraft to fall back on, and why the great post-pandemic recovery instrument, which did involve common borrowing, was treated as such a remarkable and exceptional departure. The loop closes: discharge Finally, the accountability you have been assembling across three earlier chapters clicks shut. The Commission spends the money (Chapter 6). The Court of Auditors examines the accounts and reports on whether it was spent lawfully and well (Chapter 9). And on the strength of that report, the European Parliament decides each year whether to grant the Commission discharge, its formal sign-off that the budget was properly executed (Chapter 7). Raised from customs and national wealth, planned seven years ahead, spent through two chambers, and audited and signed off by the elected one: the money of the Union travels a complete and watched circle. Why it matters So the Union\'s finances tell their own quiet story about what it is. It cannot tax you, so it leans on the members and on the tariffs of its own external border. It plans far ahead, in seven-year frameworks. It spends most on farmers and on lifting up its poorer regions. It must balance its books every year. And every euro is signed off, in the end, by the Parliament that Europe elects. Money and markets, though, are only ever means. The end they serve is the citizen, and it is to the citizen, and the rights the Union grants each one, that the next chapter turns. THE MONEY OF THE UNION The Spine of the Story REVENUE · OWN RESOURCES No direct EU tax. The budget is funded by own resources: customs duties on imports from outside the Union; a share of VAT; the GNI-based contribution (the largest, by economic size); and a plastic-packaging contribution (since 2021). The own-resources system is decided by the Council unanimously and ratified by all Member States. PLANNING & SPENDING MFF: the seven-year spending plan (ceilings by category); adopted by the Council (unanimity) with the Parliament\'s consent. Annual budget: the Commission drafts; the Parlia‐ ment and Council adopt (the budgetary authority). Main spending: chiefly agriculture (CAP) and cohesion (poorer regions); then research, external action, borders, ad‐ ministration. THE GOLDEN RULE & DISCHARGE The budget must balance every year; the Union may not bor‐ row to run a deficit. At year\'s end, the Parliament grants the Commission discharge, using the Court of Auditors\' report. ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. No direct EU tax — the Union lives on own resources. 2. The GNI-based contribution is the largest source. 3. The MFF runs seven years; Council (unanimity) + Parlia‐ ment\'s consent. 4. The annual budget is adopted by Parliament and Council together; the Commission only drafts. 5. The budget must balance every year — no deficit. 6.',
  14: 'CHAPTER 14  Citizens of the Union The second citizenship Maastricht gave every European, and the concrete, usable rights that come with it. S tand back from everything this book has built so far, the institutions, the courts, the market, the money, and ask the plainest question of all: what is it for? A parliament, a currency, a budget are only ever machinery. The thing the machinery is meant to serve is the ordinary person, and at Maastricht in 1992 the Union did something quietly radical to say so. It invented a new kind of citizen. Until then, you were a citizen of France, of Ireland, of Portugal, and nothing more. Maastricht created, on top of that, a citizen‐ ship of the Union, and it belongs to you automatically the moment you are a national of any Member State. It does not replace your national citizenship, it is added to it. And it is not an empty honorific. It comes with a bundle of concrete, usable rights that no other international arrangement on Earth grants to individuals. This chapter is the citizen\'s own toolkit. A second citizenship, added not substituted The founding rule, in Article 20 TFEU, is worth stating precisely, because the exam tests the precision. Every person holding the nationality of a Member State is a citizen of the Union. Union citizenship is additional to national citizenship and does not re‐ place it. You cannot be an EU citizen without first being a national of a member country, and becoming an EU citizen takes nothing away from your national identity. It is a second layer, not a substi‐ tute. CITIZENS OF THE UNION What that second layer gives you is a set of rights, spelled out in the treaties, that turn "citizen of the Union" from a slogan into something you can actually use. The right to move and live The first right you already know from Chapter 10: the freedom to move and reside anywhere in the Union. As a citizen you may travel, settle, work or retire in any Member State, and this is the right most Europeans feel in their bones, the one that lets a Spanish lawyer build a life in Luxembourg or a retiree from the north grow old in the sun of the south. It is the foundation on which the others build. The right to vote where you live The second right hides the single most common trap in this whole subject, so read it slowly. As a Union citizen living in a Member State other than your own, you may vote and stand as a candid‐ ate in two kinds of election there, in the country where you live, on the same terms as its own nationals: the European Parliament elections, and the municipal (local) elections. Now the trap. That right covers European and local elections only. It does not extend to that country\'s national (general) or re‐ gional elections; for those, you still vote only as a citizen of your own home country. A French citizen living in Luxembourg may help elect Luxembourg\'s MEPs and sit on its town council, but may not vote for Luxembourg\'s parliament. European and municipal, yes; national, no. VOTING WHERE YOU RESIDE (in another Member State) The right to be protected abroad The third right is one many citizens never realise they have. If you are travelling in a country outside the Union, a distant place where your own country has no embassy or consulate, you may seek dip‐ lomatic and consular protection from the embassy of any other Member State, on the same terms as its own nationals. A Maltese citizen stranded far from home, where Malta has no mission, can turn to the German or French consulate for help. In the wider world, every EU embassy is, in a real sense, partly yours. The rights to be heard The remaining rights let the citizen speak back to the Union itself. You may petition the European Parliament on a matter that af‐ fects you. You may complain to the European Ombudsman, an office elected by the Parliament to investigate maladministration by the EU\'s own institutions, the mislaid file, the unfair procedure, CITIZENS OF THE UNION the unanswered letter. You may write to any EU institution in any of the official languages and receive a reply in the same language. And, most ambitiously of all, through the European Citizens\' Initiative you may join with one million citizens drawn from at least a quarter of the Member States to formally invite the Com‐ mission to propose a new law, a rare thread of direct, participatory democracy woven into a representative system. THE CITIZEN\'S TOOLKIT EU maladministration 1 million citizens · ≥ 7 states → invite the Commission to propose a law write & be answered UNION CITIZENSHIP Why it matters So citizenship of the Union is not decoration. It is a working set of rights: to live and move freely, to vote where you reside for Europe and for your town, to be protected by any Member State\'s embassy when you are far from home, and to petition, complain, write and even help set the legislative agenda. These are the threads that tie the whole vast apparatus back down to the single human being it exists to serve. The market and the money were means; this is near‐ er the end. And standing behind all of these particular rights is a broader guarantee of fundamental rights, a Charter, which is where the next chapter goes. CITIZENS OF THE UNION The Spine of the Story 20 TFEU Every national of a Member State is automatically a citizen of the Union. It is additional to national citizenship and does not replace it. Created at Maastricht (1992). THE RIGHTS OF THE CITIZEN Move & reside Live, work, study or retire anywhere (Art 21) Vote & stand EP + municipal elections where you reside — not national (Art 22) Consular pro‐ Any Member State\'s embassy in a third Petition Petition the European Parliament (Art 227) Ombudsman Maladministration by EU institutions (Art 228) Citizens\' Initi‐ 1 million from ≥ 7 states invite the Com‐ Language Write to the institutions in any official lan‐ guage CITIZENS OF THE UNION ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Union citizenship is additional to national citizenship; it does not replace it. 2. It is automatic for nationals of a Member State — nothing to apply for. 3. Where you reside you vote in European and municipal elections only — not national ones. The top trap. 4. Consular protection: any Member State\'s embassy abroad, where your own has none. 5. The Ombudsman handles maladministration by EU insti‐ tutions, not national bodies. 6. The Citizens\' Initiative needs 1 million from ≥ 7 states; it invites the Commission, it does not force a law.',
  15: 'CHAPTER 15  The Charter of Rights The Union\'s bill of fundamental rights, how it gained the force of the Treaties, and the one question that decides when it applies. THE CHARTER OF RIGHTS T he last chapter gave the citizen a set of usable rights, to move, to vote, to be protected. But those are, in a sense, the practical rights of membership. Behind them lies a deeper question. A Union that can fine the world\'s largest companies, bind sovereign nations to its law and manage the money of a continent is itself a form of power, and any power that great must, in a decent order, be bound. What guards the fundamental human rights, dignity, liberty, equality, a fair trial, against the Union\'s own authority? For a long time the answer was awkward, because the founding treaties contained no bill of rights at all. Into that silence stepped the Court of Justice, which built a protection of fundamental rights case by case, as unwritten "general principles" of Union law. It worked, but it was scattered and hard to see. So at last, in the year 2000, the Union did the obvious thing. It wrote its rights down, in a single document: the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union. From proclamation to binding law When it was first proclaimed in 2000, the Charter was a solemn declaration but not, yet, hard law. That changed with the Treaty of Lisbon in 2009, which gave the Charter its teeth. Article 6 of the Treaty on European Union now declares that the Charter has "the same legal value as the Treaties." Read that phrase carefully, because it is the whole point: the Charter is no longer a wish-list but primary law, sitting at the very top of the hierarchy you met in Chapter 3, alongside the TEU and the TFEU themselves. What the Charter contains The Charter gathers into one text the rights that older constitu‐ tions scattered across many, and it organises them not by dry legal category but under six great values, each the title of a chapter: Dignity, Freedoms, Equality, Solidarity, Citizens\' Rights and Justice, six value-titles capped by a seventh and final title of general provisions, seven titles in all, and that seventh title is where the scope rule you are about to meet lives. Across its fifty-four articles it unites, in a single modern document, the classical civil and political rights, human dignity, the right to life, the prohibition of torture and slavery, liberty, privacy, freedom of expression and religion, with newer and more social ones, the protection of personal data, the rights of the child and the elderly, workers\' rights, and the guarantees of a fair trial. It is, in effect, the Union\'s written conscience. WHEN DOES THE CHARTER BIND? · ARTICLE 51 Not purely national matters · and it does not extend the Union\'s competences a limit on how EU law is exercised, not a new grant of power FIGURE 1. WHEN THE CHARTER BINDS — THE SCOPE RULE OF ARTICLE 51 THE CHARTER OF RIGHTS The question that decides everything: when does it bind? Now the single most tested point in this whole chapter, and the one candidates most often get wrong. It is not enough to know what the Charter says; you must know when it applies, and the answer is in Article 51. The Charter always binds the institutions and bodies of the Union themselves, in everything they do. But it binds the Member States only when they are acting within the scope of Union law, that is, when they are implementing or operating within EU law. It does not reach into purely national matters that lie outside the field of Union law, and, crucially, it does not extend the Uni‐ on\'s powers by so much as an inch. The Charter is a limit on how EU law is exercised, not a new grant of competence and not a general human-rights code for every situation in a Member State. Institutions: always. Member States: only within EU law. Purely national matters: not covered. Two systems, two cities: the Charter and the Convention One more distinction seals the chapter, and it reaches back to the traps of Chapters 5 and 8. The Charter is EU law, and its ultimate guardian is the Court of Justice in Luxembourg. It is easy to con‐ fuse with a different and older instrument, the European Convention on Human Rights, which is not an EU text at all: it belongs to the Council of Europe, and it is enforced by the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg. The two over‐ lap heavily in content, both protect dignity, liberty, a fair trial, but they are separate systems, with separate courts in separate cities. The Union has long been committed, under the Treaty, to acced‐ ing to the Convention as well, a process still unfinished. For the exam, hold the pairing firm: Charter → EU → Luxembourg; Convention → Council of Europe → Strasbourg. THE SIX VALUE-TITLES OF THE CHARTER life · integrity · no torture liberty · privacy · expression non-discrimination workers\' & social rights vote · petition · good admin fair trial · defence Why it matters So the Charter is the point at which the Union binds itself. Written down in 2000, raised to the full force of the Treaties in 2009, or‐ ganised around six human values and reaching across fifty-four articles, it disciplines the institutions in all they do and the Member States whenever they act within Union law, while leaving purely national life and national competences untouched. It is distinct from the Strasbourg Convention, though they are cousins. And it completes the answer to the question this part of the book has been asking: the vast machinery does not only serve the citizen; it is now, THE CHARTER OF RIGHTS by its own supreme law, answerable to the citizen\'s fundamental rights. The Spine of the Story RIGHTS Proclaimed 2000; became legally binding with the Treaty of Lisbon (2009), gaining "the same legal value as the Treaties" (Art 6 TEU) — it is primary law. 54 articles in seven titles: six named after values, Dignity · Freedoms · Equality · Solidarity · Citizens\' Rights · Justice, plus Title VII, General Provisions (home of Article 51). SCOPE & THE TWO SYSTEMS Scope (Art 51): binds the EU institutions always; binds the Member States only when acting within the scope of EU law. It does not cover purely national matters and does not extend the Union\'s competences. Charter vs Convention: Charter = EU law, upheld by the CJEU (Luxembourg). The European Convention on Human Rights = a Council of Europe treaty, upheld by the ECtHR (Strasbourg). The EU is committed to acceding to the Convention (Art 6(2) TEU). THE CHARTER OF RIGHTS ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Binding since Lisbon (2009); same legal value as the Treaties (primary law). 2. Scope (Art 51): institutions always; Member States only within EU law; not purely national matters. 3. The Charter does not extend the Union\'s competences. 4. Charter ≠ ECHR: Charter = EU / CJEU / Luxembourg; Convention = Council of Europe / ECtHR / Strasbourg. 5. Seven titles in all: the first six are the values (Dignity, Freedoms, Equality, Solidarity, Citizens\' Rights, Justice); Title VII holds the general provisions, including Article 51. 6. Before the Charter, rights were protected by the Court as general principles of EU law.',
  16: 'CHAPTER 16  Freedom, Security and Justice The borderless space where internal frontiers vanish, a common external border is kept, and police and courts cooperate so no criminal escapes by crossing a line. FREEDOM, SECURITY AND JUSTICE F ree movement, the great gift of Chapter 10, carried a hidden problem inside it. If honest travellers can cross a border without stopping, then so can the dishonest. Erase the frontier for the tourist and the worker, and you also erase it for the smuggler, the trafficker and the fugitive. A criminal who could once be stopped at a checkpoint can now simply drive across and vanish into another country\'s separate, sovereign legal system. A space of free movement, in other words, creates an urgent need for a matching space of security and justice, or the freedom becomes a gift to the wrong people. The Union\'s answer is a policy area with a name that says exactly what it is: the Area of Freedom, Security and Justice. The idea is to treat the whole Union, for these purposes, as a single space: internal borders open, but a common watch kept on the external frontier, shared rules on who may enter, and police and courts that cooperate closely enough that a national border is no longer a place to hide. Borders: open inside, watched outside The foundation is Schengen, which you first met in Chapter 1. Within the Schengen area, the checks at internal borders are ab‐ olished: you cross from one country to the next without showing a passport, as freely as moving between provinces of a single state. Today the area counts twenty-nine countries, twenty-five of the twenty-seven Member States (Ireland keeps an opt-out; Cyprus has not yet joined) together with four non-EU neighbours, and its newest full members are Bulgaria and Romania, in since January 2025. But a space with no internal borders can only work if its ex‐ ternal border is guarded in common, so Schengen pairs the open inside with a shared external frontier, a common Schengen visa for short visits, and a joint agency, the European Border and Coast Guard, known as Frontex, to help the Member States police the outer edge. Open on the inside, watched on the outside: that is the whole logic of the border regime. Internal border checks abolished One common external border cross as if between provinces shared asylum & migration rules OUTSIDE — THE LOGIC OF SCHENGEN Asylum and migration in common Once internal borders vanish, a second thing must be shared: the rules on who may come in and stay. If each country ran an en‐ tirely separate asylum policy, a person refused in one could simply walk into the next, and the whole system would unravel. So the Union has built a Common European Asylum System, with shared standards and rules for deciding which Member State is responsible for examining a given asylum claim, alongside a common approach to legal migration and to the return of those FREEDOM, SECURITY AND JUSTICE with no right to remain. Migration and asylum are, in this area, a shared responsibility, not twenty-seven separate ones. Justice: cooperation built on mutual trust The third pillar is the one that most directly stops the border from becoming a hiding place, and it rests on a single principle: mutual recognition, built on mutual trust. Because the Member States trust one another\'s courts, a judicial decision taken in one can be recognised and acted upon in another, almost as if there were no border between their legal systems. The flagship of this is the European Arrest Warrant. Before it, extraditing a suspect from one country to another was a slow, political, often years-long ordeal. The Arrest Warrant swept that away: a warrant issued by a judge in one Member State must, as a rule, be executed across the whole Union, so that a suspect who flees abroad can be swiftly surrendered back to face justice. A border no longer buys a fugitive years of delay; often it buys only days. Around this sit the Union\'s justice and policing bodies, and their names and jobs are worth learning as a set. Europol is the Union\'s agency for police cooperation, pooling intelligence and coordinating cross-border investigations. Eurojust does the same for the prosecutors, coordinating the work of national prosecuting authorities across borders. And the newest, the European Public Prosecutor\'s Office, the EPPO, goes a decisive step further: it is an independent Union prosecutor that can itself investigate and pro‐ secute crimes against the Union\'s own financial interests, fraud against the EU budget, giving Europe, for the first time, a prosecutor of its own. MUTUAL RECOGNITION · COOPERATION IN JUSTICE Fast surrender of suspects across the Union Police cooperation & shared intelligence FIGURE 2. THE TOOLS OF JUDICIAL AND POLICE COOPERA‐ TION Why it matters So the borderless space of free movement is answered, deliberately, by a borderless space of security and justice. The internal frontiers come down, but a common external border goes up in their place, watched together through Schengen and Frontex; the rules on asylum and migration are shared rather than splintered; and, through mutual recognition, the European Arrest Warrant, Europol, Eurojust and the EPPO, police forces and courts reach across the vanished borders so that justice can follow a criminal wherever in the Union they run. The freedom to move and the assurance of security are, in the end, two halves of the same design. FREEDOM, SECURITY AND JUSTICE The Spine of the Story THE AREA OF FREEDOM, SECURITY AND JUSTICE The Union as a single space: open internal borders, a com‐ mon external frontier, shared asylum/migration rules, and cooperation in justice and policing. Borders: Schengen abol‐ ishes checks at internal borders, paired with a common external border, a Schengen visa, and Frontex (the European Border and Coast Guard). Twenty-nine countries today; Bul‐ garia and Romania became full members in January 2025. Asylum & migration: a Common European Asylum Sys‐ tem; a shared competence. JUSTICE · MUTUAL RECOGNITION European Arrest Fast surrender of suspects (replaced ex‐ Europol EU agency for police cooperation and intelligence across borders EU\'s financial interests All of it rests on mutual recognition, built on mutual trust. FREEDOM, SECURITY AND JUSTICE ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Schengen abolishes internal border checks; paired with a common external border and Frontex. 2. The European Arrest Warrant = fast surrender, repla‐ cing slow extradition; rests on mutual recognition. 3. Europol = police; Eurojust = prosecutors. Do not swap them. 4. The EPPO prosecutes crimes against the EU\'s financial interests. 5. Asylum & migration are a shared competence (Com‐ mon European Asylum System). 6. The whole area rests on mutual recognition and mutual trust.',
  17: 'CHAPTER 17  The Union in the World How twenty-seven states speak with one voice on trade — and struggle to speak with one on foreign policy. THE UNION IN THE WORLD T he European project began by looking inward. Its first ambition was modest and domestic: to bind old enemies together through a shared market, to make war among neighbours not so much forbidden as pointless. But a union of twenty-seven states, four hundred and fifty million people and the largest single market on Earth cannot stay a purely inward thing. Whether it wishes to or not, it is a glob‐ al power. The only real question is whether it can act like one, with a single will, or whether it remains twenty-seven wills in a shared coat. The honest answer is: it depends on the subject. On one great field, trade, the Union speaks with a single, formidable voice. On another, foreign policy and defence, it speaks, when it can agree at all, as twenty-seven. Understanding the Union in the world means understanding those two very different voices. The single voice: trade Start with the Union\'s strongest external arm, the common com‐ mercial policy, its trade policy. Here the Union acts as one. Trade is an exclusive competence, one of the handful you met in Chapter 4, which means the Member States have handed the whole subject over. It is the Commission that negotiates trade agreements, on behalf of all twenty-seven at once, and a single common external tariff that faces the outside world. The effect is enormous. When a foreign power, however large, sits down to negotiate access to the European market, it faces not one country but the combined weight of the entire Union, the biggest market any exporter could want to reach. No single Member State could command that leverage alone; together, through the Commission, they are a trading superpower. On trade, "Europe" is not a metaphor. It is a negotiator with one seat and one signature. The many voices: foreign and security policy Now turn to the other field, and the contrast is stark. The Com‐ mon Foreign and Security Policy, the CFSP, is a different animal altogether. It is largely intergovernmental: decisions are taken mostly by unanimity, in the European Council and the Council, which means that any single Member State can block a common position. It does not run through the ordinary legislative procedure of Chapter 7; there is no co-decision here, and the Court of Justice has almost no role. Foreign policy is coordinated among the states, not commanded from the centre. This is why "Europe" can seem slow, divided or silent when a crisis erupts. It is not a flaw anyone forgot to fix; it is a deliberate choice. War and peace, recognition and sanction, touch the very heart of national sovereignty, and the states have kept a firm hand on them. On foreign policy, the single voice of trade gives way to a chorus that must first agree. THE UNION IN THE WORLD An exclusive competence Largely intergovernmental The Commission negotiates for all 27 Decided mainly by unanimity a trading superpower — coordinated among states, one seat, one signature not commanded from the centre FIGURE 1. TWO VOICES — THE UNION SPEAKS AS ONE ON TRADE, AS MANY ON FOREIGN POLICY The face and the service Even a chorus needs a conductor, and the Union has one: the High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy. This figure is worth learning precisely, because the exam loves the double hat. The High Representative is at once a Vice- President of the Commission and the chair of the Foreign Affairs Council of ministers, straddling the supranational and the intergovernmental sides of the Union, and it is the High Repres‐ entative who conducts the CFSP and speaks for the Union in the wider world. Behind that single figure stands a whole diplomatic corps: the European External Action Service, the EEAS, the Union\'s own foreign service, with delegations, in effect embassies, in capitals across the globe. The Union, in other words, has diplomats of its own. Defence, aid and soft power Two more threads complete the picture. Woven into the CFSP is the Common Security and Defence Policy, under which the Uni‐ on mounts civilian and military missions abroad, peacekeeping, training, crisis management, though for the territorial defence of most members, the North Atlantic alliance remains central. And beyond the hard edges of policy lies the Union\'s greatest quiet strength: together, the EU and its Member States are the world\'s largest provider of development and humanitarian aid. Add the pull of its market, the reach of its standards and the weight of its sanctions, and much of the Union\'s real influence in the world turns out to be soft power, the power to attract and to set the rules, rather than the power to compel. THE UNION\'S EXTERNAL TOOLKIT tariffs (Commission) delegations abroad (NATO still central) development donor (soft power) Why it matters So the Union in the world is a study in two speeds. On trade, it is already a giant that acts as one, negotiating for a continent with a single signature. On foreign and security policy, it is a work in progress, a union of sovereign wills that can act powerfully when it agrees and fall silent when it does not. The High Representative THE UNION IN THE WORLD and the EEAS give it a face and a diplomatic service; trade gives it genuine muscle; aid and standards give it reach. Whether the many voices ever fully become one is the great unfinished question of the Union\'s global future, and it is bound up with a final pair of questions this book must answer: who gets to join this Union, and who may leave it. The Spine of the Story TWO VOICES On trade, the Union acts as one; on foreign policy, as many. Trade (common commercial policy): an exclusive compet‐ ence; the Commission negotiates for all 27 behind a single common external tariff; a trading superpower. Foreign policy (CFSP): largely intergovernmental, decided mainly by unanimity; no ordinary legislative procedure; any state can block. The High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy is double-hatted: a Vice-President of the Commis‐ sion and chair of the Foreign Affairs Council; backed by the EEAS (the EU\'s diplomatic service) and EU delegations abroad. The CSDP runs missions (NATO still central for defence); the EU + members are the world\'s largest aid donor; much influence is soft power. THE UNION IN THE WORLD ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Trade is exclusive; the Commission negotiates for all 27. Foreign policy is intergovernmental. 2. The CFSP is decided mainly by unanimity and does not use the ordinary legislative procedure. 3. The High Representative is double-hatted: VP of the Commission and chair of the Foreign Affairs Council. 4. The EEAS is the EU\'s diplomatic service; delegations are its embassies. 5. The EU + members are the world\'s largest aid donor. 6. Defence: the CSDP runs missions, but NATO remains central for most members.',
  18: 'CHAPTER 18  The Open Door How a country joins the Union — the values and the Copenhagen criteria — and how, since Lisbon, one can also leave. THE OPEN DOOR T he European Union is not a fixed and finished club. It began, in Chapter 1, as six states, and it has grown, wave upon wave, to twenty-seven, and once, in a mo‐ ment few had thought possible, it shrank by one. There is, in other words, a door in the wall of the Union, and it turns out to swing both ways. This final chapter of the story is about that door: the long and demanding path in, and the newer, once-unthinkable path out. The way in: Article 49 and the price of entry The path to membership begins in Article 49 of the Treaty on European Union, and it opens with two conditions that echo the very first chapters of this book. To apply, a state must be European, and it must respect, and be committed to promoting, the Union\'s values, the values of Chapter 2: dignity, freedom, democracy, equality, the rule of law and human rights. A country that does not share those values need not knock; the door is shut to it from the start. But sharing the values only lets a country apply. To actually join, it must clear a far higher bar, a checklist agreed in 1993 and ever since known as the Copenhagen criteria. There are three of them, and they are worth learning as a trio. The first is political: the applicant must have stable institu‐ tions guaranteeing democracy, the rule of law, human rights and the protection of minorities. The second is economic: it must have a functioning market economy, robust enough to cope with the competitive pressure of the single market. And the third is the hardest of all, the criterion of the acquis: the applicant must be able to take on all the obligations of membership, which means adopting the entire body of existing EU law, the acquis commun‐ autaire, tens of thousands of pages built up over seventy years, and making it work at home. To these three is added a fourth consideration on the Union\'s own side: its capacity to absorb a new member without losing its footing. THE COPENHAGEN CRITERIA · TO JOIN THE ROAD IN Apply → Candidate → Negotiate the acquis → Accession Treaty (ratified by all) IA AND THE ROAD TO ACCESSION The long road of accession Meeting that bar is the work of years. A state first lodges its applic‐ ation; if accepted in principle, it becomes an official candidate; then begin the accession negotiations, in which the vast acquis is worked through, area by area, chapter by chapter, until the candidate\'s laws and institutions are brought into line. Only when all of that is done is an Accession Treaty signed, and even then it must be ratified by every existing Member State and by the new‐ comer, each in its own way. There is no shortcut into the Union. THE OPEN DOOR The door opens slowly, and only to those who have genuinely done the work. The way out: Article 50 For most of its history, the Union had no formal way to leave at all; the treaties were silent, and departure was simply not contemplated. That changed with the Treaty of Lisbon, which for the first time wrote an exit into the law: Article 50 TEU. Its logic is the mirror image of entry. Any Member State may decide to withdraw from the Union in accordance with its own constitutional requirements, its own democratic process, not the Union\'s permission. Once it has decided, it notifies the European Council, and a withdrawal agreement is negotiated to settle the terms of the parting. A clock then starts: the treaties cease to apply either when that agreement enters into force or, failing one, two years after the notification, a deadline that can be extended only by unanimous agreement. For decades this article was a purely theoretical possibility. Then, with the departure of the United Kingdom, "Brexit," it became the first and so far only time the door has been used to walk out. ARTICLE 50 OUT Two doors, one wall So the Union\'s membership is neither automatic nor irreversible, and the symmetry is elegant. To come in, under Article 49, a state must be European, share the values, and satisfy the demanding Copenhagen criteria, adopting the whole acquis before an acces‐ sion treaty ratified by all. To go out, under Article 50, a state need only decide, by its own constitution, and then negotiate its exit within a two-year window. A high and value-guarded threshold to enter; a sovereign, lawful path to leave. Why it matters The open door is, in the end, a statement about what the Union is. That entry is so demanding, and guarded so firmly by the values, says the Union is a community of shared principles and not merely a trade arrangement; you cannot buy your way in, you must be‐ come the kind of state that belongs. And that exit exists at all, since Lisbon, says the Union is a union of free nations and not a cage; membership is a choice that can, in the last resort, be unmade. A THE OPEN DOOR high threshold and an open exit: together they describe a Union that one joins by conviction and remains in by consent. The Spine of the Story To apply: be a European state that respects and promotes the Union\'s values. To join: meet the Copenhagen criteria (1993) — political (stable democracy, rule of law, human rights, minorities); economic (a functioning market economy able to cope with competition); acquis (take on all obligations, adopting the acquis communautaire). Plus the Union\'s ab‐ sorption capacity. Process: application → candidate → negotiations → Acces‐ sion Treaty, ratified by all. Introduced by Lisbon. A Member State may withdraw in ac‐ cordance with its own constitutional requirements; it noti‐ fies the European Council; a withdrawal agreement is nego‐ tiated; the treaties cease to apply on that agreement or, failing one, two years after notification (extendable by unanimity). The United Kingdom (Brexit) is the only example so far. THE OPEN DOOR ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Article 49 = joining; Article 50 = leaving. Do not swap the numbers. 2. Applying needs values + being European; joining needs the Copenhagen criteria. 3. Three Copenhagen criteria: political, economic, and the acquis. 4. The acquis communautaire is the whole existing body of EU law. 5. An Accession Treaty must be ratified by every Member State. 6. Withdrawal (Art 50) was introduced by Lisbon; a two- year window (extendable).',
  19: 'CHAPTER 19  The Union That Moves How the Treaties are rewritten, and how a Union of twenty-seven can let some members go faster than others. A Union built on treaties carries a permanent tension inside it. A treaty is a fixed text, a contract signed and sealed; but the world it governs never stops moving. New problems arrive, old rules chafe, and the Union must somehow change with the times, even though changing a contract signed by twenty-seven sovereign states is one of the hardest political feats imaginable. So the question this short chapter answers is a simple one with a subtle answer: how does the Union change? There are two answers, and they are quite different in character. The Union can rewrite its own founding treaties, through a de‐ manding, high-bar procedure. And, when the members cannot all agree, it can allow the willing to move ahead of the reluctant, let‐ ting integration proceed at more than one speed. Change from within, and movement at different speeds. Rewriting the treaties The founding rule for changing the Treaties is Article 48 TEU, and its ordinary revision procedure is deliberately steep. A pro‐ posal may come from a Member State\'s government, the Parlia‐ ment or the Commission; it goes to the European Council, which decides to examine it; usually a Convention is convened, bringing together national parliaments, heads of state or government, the Parliament and the Commission, to thrash out the amendments, followed by a formal conference of the governments. But here is the decisive step, the one that makes treaty change so rare and so momentous. Whatever is agreed must then be ratified by every single Member State, each according to its own consti‐ tutional requirements, which in some countries means a parlia‐ mentary vote and in others a national referendum. Twenty-seven separate ratifications, any one of which can fail. It is why a full treaty revision is a generational event, not a routine adjustment. Alongside this steep path, the Treaties also allow simplified pro‐ cedures for lesser changes and the so-called passerelle (bridging) clauses, which can, for instance, shift a policy from unanimous voting to majority voting, but even these come hedged with safeguards, and none may increase the Union\'s competences. gov / EP / Commission + Convention / IGC by the governments each per its constitution (some by referendum) REVISION PROCEDURE (ARTICLE 48) When not everyone agrees: enhanced cooperation The demand for unanimity has an obvious danger: it lets any one government freeze the whole Union in place. If every step forward required all twenty-seven to move together, a single reluctant member could halt progress for everyone. So the Treaties built in a safety valve, called enhanced cooperation. Under it, a group of at least nine Member States may press ahead and cooperate more closely among themselves, within the Union\'s own legal framework, when it proves impossible for the Union as a whole to act. Crucially, the door is always left open: any other Member State may join the group later, once it is ready or willing. Enhanced cooperation lets the keen advance without either forcing or abandoning the cautious. It is how the Union moves when it cannot move as one. A Union of many speeds The natural result of all this is that the Union is not uniform, and never has been. Twenty-one countries use the euro (Bulgaria the newest, since 1 January 2026) and six do not; most are in Schengen and a few are outside; several hold formal opt-outs from particular policies. This patchwork has many names, variable geometry, dif‐ ferentiated integration, a multi-speed Europe, but the idea be‐ hind them is the same: a Union diverse enough to contain twenty- seven very different nations can only deepen if it does not insist that every member move in perfect lockstep. The euro area and the Schengen zone, which you have met throughout this book, are themselves the largest living examples of a Union that lets its members integrate at different speeds. A UNION OF MANY SPEEDS ALL 27 MEMBER STATES Why it matters So the Union is neither frozen nor uniform, and that is by design. It can rewrite its own constitution, but only through a procedure so demanding, unanimous agreement and ratification by all, that it does so rarely and deliberately. And it can let the willing go faster than the reluctant, through enhanced cooperation and the wider tolerance of a multi-speed Europe. Change from within and movement at different speeds are the two ways a treaty-based union of sovereign states keeps evolving without either standing still or breaking apart. It bends so that it need not break. The Spine of the Story TEU The ordinary revision procedure: proposal → European Council → usually a Convention → intergovernmental con‐ ference → agreement → ratified by all Member States (each per its constitution; sometimes by referendum). Also simpli‐ fied procedures and passerelle (bridging) clauses (e.g., unan‐ imity → majority voting), hedged with safeguards; none may increase the Union\'s competences. SPEEDS Enhanced cooperation: a group of at least nine Member States may cooperate more closely among themselves, within the Union\'s framework, when the Union as a whole cannot act; always open to others. Differentiated integration ("vari‐ able geometry," "multi-speed Europe"): the Union is not uniform (euro area, Schengen, opt-outs); members may integrate at different speeds. ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Treaty revision (Art 48) requires ratification by all — unanimity plus universal ratification. 2. Simplified revision & passerelle clauses may not increase EU competences. 3. Enhanced cooperation needs at least nine Member States and stays open. 4. The euro area and Schengen are examples of differentiated integration. 5. A passerelle can shift a policy from unanimity to qualified-majority voting. 6. Treaty change is rare because any one national ratification can block it.',
  20: 'CHAPTER 20  The Democratic Life How a Union of institutions stays a Union of citizens — representation, participation, and the demand for openness. O ne criticism has shadowed the European project for as long as it has existed, and any honest account must meet it head-on. It is the charge of a "demo‐ cratic deficit": the worry that the Union\'s decisions are taken too far away, by institutions too remote, for ordinary citizens to feel that they truly govern themselves. A continent-sized machine that can bind nations and fine giants, the fear goes, may slip beyond the reach of the very people it serves. The Treaties take that worry seriously, and answer it deliber‐ ately. Woven through the Treaty on European Union is a set of democratic principles, and they describe a Union that tries to stay, for all its size, a Union of citizens. The answer comes in layers: two channels of representation, several avenues of direct participation, the vigilance of national parliaments, and a demand for openness. This closing chapter of the story gathers them together. Represented twice over The backbone of it all is representative democracy, and the Uni‐ on\'s design gives every citizen not one but two channels of repres‐ entation, a point the exam likes to test. The first channel is direct: you elect, yourself, the members of the European Parliament (Chapter 7), the only EU institution chosen at the ballot box. The second is indirect: your national government, which you also elect, sits in the European Council and the Council of the Union (Chapter 8), and that government answers, at home, to your national parliament. So the citizen is present in the Union twice, once through the Parliament directly, and once through a national government held to account by a national parliament. Two threads, both leading back to the voter. TWO CHANNELS OF REPRESENTATION CITIZEN directly elected — the only one CITIZEN The right to take part Representation is not the whole of democracy, though, and the Treaties add a second mode: participatory democracy, the cit‐ izen\'s right not merely to be represented but to take part. The in‐ stitutions are bound to keep an open dialogue with citizens and civil society and to consult widely before acting. And, most con‐ cretely, there is the instrument you met in Chapter 14, the European Citizens\' Initiative, by which a million citizens, drawn from a minimum number of Member States, can formally invite the Commission to propose a law. Democracy here is not only a matter of choosing representatives every few years; it is meant to be an ongoing conversation. The sentinels: national parliaments There is a third guardian, and it sits, cleverly, inside the national capitals rather than in Brussels. The national parliaments are giv‐ en a formal watchdog role over the Union, and above all over sub‐ sidiarity, the principle from Chapter 4 that the Union should act only where it genuinely adds value over the member states. Draft EU laws are sent to every national parliament, and if enough of them object that a proposal oversteps, they can raise the "yellow card" or "orange card", obliging the Commission to think again. The national parliaments are, in effect, the member states\' demo‐ cratic sentinels posted within the Union\'s own law-making. Openness as democratic hygiene Finally, democracy needs light, and the Union builds in several sources of it. There is a right of transparency and access to docu‐ ments: citizens may, as a rule, ask to see the documents of the institutions, so that power is exercised in view rather than in shadow. There is multilingualism, the Union conducts its busi‐ ness and publishes its law in twenty-four official languages, so that no citizen is shut out of European law simply for not speaking a foreign tongue. And there is the European Ombudsman (Chapter 14), who investigates complaints of maladministration in the institutions, giving the ordinary citizen a champion against bureaucratic wrong. Openness, language and redress: the quiet plumbing of a democratic order. HOW THE CITIZEN CONNECTS elect the European Parliament 1 million invite the Commission be heard; challenge maladministration guard subsidiarity (yellow card) access to the institutions\' documents EU law in your own tongue Why it matters So the answer to the democratic-deficit worry is not a single grand mechanism but a layering of them. You are represented directly in the Parliament and indirectly through a nationally accountable government; you may take part through initiative, petition and consultation; your national parliament stands sentinel over subsidiarity; and the institutions must work in the open, in your language, answerable to an Ombudsman. Whether all of this fully closes the deficit is a question Europeans still argue over, honestly and loudly, and that argument is itself a sign of democratic life. But the design intention is unmistakable: however vast the machine has grown, it is built to remain, in the end, answerable to its citizens. The Spine of the Story The "democratic deficit" — the worry that EU decisions are too remote. The Treaties answer with democratic principles (Title II TEU). Representative democracy — two channels: direct (citizens elect the European Parliament, the only dir‐ ectly elected institution) and indirect (national governments in the European Council and Council, accountable to national parliaments). Participatory democracy: open dialogue, consultation, and the European Citizens\' Initiative (1 million citizens invite the Commission to act). GUARDIANS & OPENNESS National parliaments are watchdogs, above all over subsidi‐ arity (the yellow/orange card, Chapter 4). Openness: trans‐ parency / access to documents; 24 official languages; the European Ombudsman (investigates maladministration). ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS 1. Citizens are represented twice: directly (Parliament) and indirectly (governments in the Council). 2. The European Parliament is the only directly elected EU institution. 3. National parliaments guard subsidiarity (the yellow/or‐ ange card). 4. The EU works in 24 official languages. 5. The Citizens\' Initiative = 1 million citizens invite the Commission to propose a law. 6. The Ombudsman investigates maladministration; transparency includes access to documents.',
  21: 'CHAPTER 21  The Agenda of the Age What the Union is doing with its powers right now — a continent changing its weather, writing the rules of the digital world, borrowing to rebuild, and opening the door once more. ENLARGEMENT RECOVERY DIGITAL GREEN THE AGENDA OF THE AGE E very generation of the Union has had one great project that defined it. For the founders it was coal and steel, the taming of the two industries of war. For the generation of Jacques Delors it was the single market, 1992 painted on every briefing in Brussels. At the turn of the millennium it was the euro, notes and coins crossing borders that armies once crossed. You have now walked through all of that history, the legal order it produced, the institutions that run it and the policies that carried it. So one last question remains before we gather everything for revision, and it is the question any curious citizen, and certainly any examiner, will ask: what is the Union doing with all of it now? The Notice of Competition promises questions on the Union\'s main policies, and the main policies of this decade have names. This chapter tells their story: the green transition, the digital rulebook, the recovery fund, the two great spending blocks that quietly carry half the budget, and the door to membership that history has pushed open again. A continent decides to change its weather In December 2019, in her first weeks in office, Commission President Ursula von der Leyen stood before the Parliament and announced what she called Europe\'s "man on the moon moment": the European Green Deal. Strip away the rhetoric and the idea is startlingly concrete. The European Union would become the first climate-neutral continent by 2050, emitting no more greenhouse gases than it removes, and it would treat this not as an environmental programme bolted onto the economy but as the Union\'s growth strategy itself, redirecting investment, industry, energy, transport and farming toward one destination. Announcements are politics; what makes this one exam material is that the Union did something very characteristic with it. It turned the promise into law. The European Climate Law, Reg‐ ulation (EU) 2021/1119, adopted in 2021 through the ordinary legislative procedure you learned in the institutional chapters, makes climate neutrality by 2050 a legally binding obligation, not an aspiration, and fixes a binding staging post on the way: cutting net emissions by at least 55% by 2030, measured against 1990 levels. The vast package of legislation adopted to hit that number was named after it, "Fit for 55". Hold the two dates the way an examiner will test them: 55% is 2030; neutrality is 2050. Swapping them is the oldest trap in this topic. The Union\'s favourite tool here is also its oldest market trick. The Emissions Trading System, the ETS, running since 2005 and the world\'s first major carbon market, does not tax carbon; it caps the total quantity of emissions allowed and lets companies trade the permits within that shrinking cap, so the price of polluting is set by a market the law created. And because a continent that prices carbon fears imports from places that do not, the Union added the Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism, CBAM, in 2023: car‐ bon-intensive goods entering the Union pay at the border what they would have paid inside it. Cap-and-trade at home, a carbon price at the door: the single market, once again, exporting its rules. THE AGENDA OF THE AGE Writing the rules of the digital world The second agenda began with a quiet regulation about personal data that turned out to be heard around the world. The General Data Protection Regulation, the GDPR, adopted in 2016 and applying since 2018, gave individuals enforceable rights over their personal data and backed them with fines large enough to reach the biggest companies on earth. Because those companies found it easier to apply one strict standard everywhere than to run separate systems, the GDPR became a de facto global benchmark, the phenomenon observers call the "Brussels effect": the Union regu‐ lates its own market, and the world adjusts. In 2022 the Union followed with a matched pair of laws whose names the exam loves to swap. The Digital Services Act, the DSA, is about content and responsibility: it obliges online platforms, and especially the very largest, to tackle illegal content and goods, to be transparent about their algorithms and advertising, and to assess the risks their services create for society. The Digital Markets Act, the DMA, is about competition before the fact: it designates the handful of giant "gatekeeper" platforms and imposes duties on them in advance, no self-preferencing, no locking users in, interoperability where it matters, so that the referee of Chapter 11 does not have to wait years for an abuse case to conclude. One sentence to keep them straight: the DSA polices what platforms host; the DMA polices how gatekeepers compete. Then, in 2024, the Union did it again on the newest frontier. The Artificial Intelligence Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, is the world\'s first comprehensive law on artificial intelligence, and its design is worth ten seconds of your memory because it is elegant: a risk pyramid. A narrow set of uses judged incompatible with fundamental rights, such as social scoring by public authorities, is prohibited outright; high-risk systems, in areas like hiring, credit or justice, face strict requirements before and after they reach the market; limited-risk systems owe transparency, a chatbot must say it is a chatbot; and minimal-risk uses are left alone. Not a ban on AI, and not a shrug: obligations proportionate to risk, which is, when you think about it, subsidiarity\'s cousin applied to technology. THE TWIN TRANSITIONS GREEN DIGITAL Two ladders, one method: turn a political agenda into binding law, then let the market carry it. DOES. The debt that built a recovery When the pandemic closed the continent in 2020, the Union faced the sharpest recession in its history with a budget that, as you learned in Chapter 13, must balance and had never been allowed to THE AGENDA OF THE AGE borrow at scale. What happened next is the kind of institutional plot twist this book exists to make memorable. In July 2020, after one of the longest European Council summits ever held, the Member States agreed to let the Commission borrow on the capital markets on behalf of the Union: up to €750 billion in 2018 prices, roughly €800 billion in the money of the day, under the name NextGenerationEU, NGEU. The frugal chapters of this book still apply, the annual budget still balances, but sitting beside it, temporary and extraordinary, is the largest common borrowing operation in the Union\'s history. The money flows mainly through the Recovery and Resilience Facility, the RRF, and the mechanism matters more than the sum. Member States do not simply receive cheques. Each submitted a national recovery and resilience plan, and money is paid out in instalments, against milestones and targets: reforms passed, in‐ vestments delivered. And the plans had to lean into the twin transitions, with minimum shares of spending for climate (at least 37%) and digital (at least 20%) objectives. Repayment stretches decades ahead, through future budgets and proposed new own resources, which is why NGEU also quietly reshaped the budget chapter you already know.',
  22: 'CHAPTER 22  The Whole Union at a Glance The tower view — every quarter of the city you have crossed, laid out at once, and the hard facts gathered onto a single table. T here is a moment, near the end of any long journey, when it helps to stop walking the streets and climb instead to the highest tower, so that the whole city, every quarter you have crossed on foot, lies spread out at once beneath you. You have walked the European Union street by street across twenty-one chapters. Now climb the tower, and look at all of it together. The journey in one breath It began, in Part I, with a paradox: that enemies built a union, turning the coal and steel of war into the first threads of peace, and stitching them, treaty by treaty, from six states into twenty-seven, around a shared creed of values and objectives. In Part II you learned what kind of thing this Union is in law: a new legal order, resting on its founding Treaties, whose law claims primacy over national law and can take direct effect in the citizen\'s hands; a Union of powers on loan, holding only the competences its members have conferred, disciplined by subsidiarity and propor‐ tionality. Part III walked the great machine itself, the seven institutions: the elected Parliament, the summit European Council, the minis‐ ters\' Council, the executive Commission, the Court of Justice that guards the law, the Central Bank that guards the euro, and the Court of Auditors that guards the money. Part IV followed what that machine produces: a single market of four freedoms, policed by a fierce competition law, crowned by a single currency and funded by a budget with no borrowing and no direct tax. Part V turned to the citizen: the citizenship of the Union, the Charter that binds the Union\'s own power, and the borderless area of free‐ dom, security and justice. And Part VI carried the Union out into the world, and asked how it joins and leaves, how it changes, and how it stays, for all its size, a democratic life. That is the whole story, in one breath. What remains, in this chapter, is to lay the hard facts out on a single table, so that everything you will need on exam day sits in one place. THE SEVEN INSTITUTIONS · ARTICLE 13 TEU the political quartet above · the three guardians below WHERE THEY SIT THE TREATIES IN ORDER (Amsterdam 1997 and Nice 2001 sit between Maastricht and Lisbon) Why it matters The exam does not test whether you can feel the story; it tests whether you can recall the facts the story was built to make mem‐ orable. So use the tables below as your final map. If a single line of them feels unfamiliar, you know exactly which chapter to revisit. The tower view is not new learning; it is the moment the whole city finally snaps into focus. The Whole Union on One Table THE SEVEN INSTITUTIONS (ART 13 TEU) European Par‐ Directly elected co-legis‐ Strasbourg · Council ment; direction; no legis‐ lation Council of the Ministers; co-legislator; Brussels Treaties Justice (CJEU) served; primacy; prelimin‐ ary rulings European Monetary policy; price sta‐ Frankfurt Central Bank bility; authorises the euro Luxembourg Court of Aud‐ External auditor of EU Brussels Commission · Council · European Council · EEAS Stras‐ Parliament (seat) · Ombudsman — and Council bourg of Europe & ECtHR (non-EU) Luxem‐ CJEU · Court of Auditors · EPPO bourg Frankfurt ECB The Hag‐ Europol · Eurojust ue Warsaw Frontex 1986 Single European Drove the single market Act 1992/93 Maastricht Created the EU, citizenship, the euro path today\'s structure THE LANDMARK CASES & THE NUMBERS Cassis de Dijon 1979 Mutual recognition (goods) Euro area (Bulgaria joined 1 Jan 21 2026) MEPs · cap · per-state range 720 · 751 · 6–96 Qualified majority 55% states + 65% popula‐ tion Citizens\' Initiative · enhanced 1 million/≥7 · ≥9 states coop. Deficit · debt · Art 50 window ≤3% · ≤60% · 2 years ⚠ THE CLASSIC TRAPS (MASTER LIST) 1. Three "Councils": European Council · Council of the EU · Council of Europe (not EU). 2. Charter = EU/CJEU/Luxembourg; ECHR = Council of Europe/ECtHR/Strasbourg. 3. Only the Parliament is directly elected. 4. The Commission alone proposes laws. 5. Article 49 = join; Article 50 = leave. 6. Trade & competition are exclusive competences. 7. Primacy = Costa (1964); direct effect = Van Gend (1963). 8. The Court of Auditors audits — it is not a tribunal. 9. QMV = 55% of states + 65% of population. 10. The seven institutions are set by Article 13 TEU.',
  23: 'CHAPTER 23  Into the Exam How to spend forty minutes, and everything you have learned, and walk out with the marks. INTO THE EXAM Y ou have done the hard part. Across twenty-one chapters you have built, brick by brick, the knowledge this exam is looking for, and in the last chapter you climbed the tower and saw all of it at once. This final, short chapter is not about learning anything new. It is about how to spend what you already have, wisely, in the handful of minutes the exam gives you. Knowledge wins the marks; but on the day, method decides whether you get to use your knowledge at all. What you are walking into The EU-knowledge component is a multiple-choice test, taken against the clock, in your second language, as part of the AD5 as‐ sessment. It rewards fast, accurate recall of exactly the material in this book: the institutions, the treaties, the legal order, the competences, the freedoms, the citizen and the Union in the world. One word of housekeeping, and for once it is good news rather than guesswork. For this competition the format is fixed in black and white by the official Notice of Competition EPSO/AD/ 427/26 (Official Journal C/2026/711): the EU knowledge test has exactly 30 questions in 40 minutes, is taken in your language 2, and has a pass mark of 15 out of 30. It weighs 30% of the prelim‐ inary ranking and 25% of the final score, second only to verbal reasoning (40% and 35%), with digital skills at the same 30% and 25% and the EUFTE adding a final 15%; the numerical and abstract tests are pass-or-fail gates that carry no ranking weight at all. There are 1,490 places on the reserve list. And since the amendment of 30 June 2026, the competition runs in two parts: the reasoning tests come first, expected from October or Novem‐ ber 2026, and only candidates who pass them are invited to Part 2, the EU knowledge test, the digital skills test and the EUFTE, in 2027, on dates still to be announced. EPSO has already amended the notice once, so keep checking your candidate account; the Notice remains the final word. One more date to circle. EPSO has said it will publish the refer‐ ences to the sources used to develop the EU knowledge test on its website approximately two months before the test date. Read that list the day it appears, and check every chapter of this book against it: the story you now know was built precisely to make those sources stick. And notice what the two-part calendar quietly hands you: while others sprint through reasoning and only then look up, the months between Part 1 and Part 2 are the window in which EU knowledge, a quarter of your final score, is won. THE FORTY-MINUTE PLAN keep a hard ceiling move on, return later a guess beats nothing every empty box FIGURE 1. A SIMPLE PLAN FOR THE CLOCK Five habits that save marks First, budget the clock before you start. Divide the minutes by the questions and you have your rhythm: forty minutes for thirty questions is eighty seconds each. Keep a rough ceiling in your INTO THE EXAM head, and the moment a question exceeds it, stop. A hard question is worth exactly the same single mark as an easy one, so never let one puzzle devour the time of three certainties. Second, answer every question. The Notice scores this test from 0 to 30, one point a question, and provides no negative marking; EPSO\'s multiple-choice tests do not punish a wrong answer, so a blank is simply a mark you refused to try for. The rule is absolute: never leave anything empty. If the clock is running out, spend the last thirty seconds filling every remaining box with your best guess. Third, read the stem like a lawyer. The examiners\' favourite tools are the small words that flip a meaning: NOT, EXCEPT, only, always, never. Half of all wrong answers are right answers to a question that was not actually asked. Read the stem twice if it contains a negative, and be suspicious of any option stated as an ab‐ solute. Fourth, eliminate before you choose. Rarely will all four options tempt you. Kill the two that are clearly wrong, and you have turned a one-in-four guess into a one-in-two decision, even before your knowledge speaks. Then let recall settle the survivors, and trust your first instinct on a fact you know; second-guessing a solid memory is how good answers get talked into bad ones. Fifth, know the trap families cold, because they recur every year. The three Councils and the geography of who sits where; the Charter against the Convention; Article 49 in against Article 50 out; primacy against direct effect; exclusive against shared com‐ petences; who proposes a law against who decides it. If you can feel these traps coming, they stop being traps and become free marks. Where the marks cluster Not all topics appear with equal weight, and it pays to know where the questions gather. The densest zones, the ones almost certain to appear, are the institutions and their seats; the treaty timeline; primacy, direct effect and the landmark cases; the competences and subsidiarity; the four freedoms; citizenship and the Charter; and the decision-making machinery of co-decision and qualified-majority voting. If your revision time is short, spend it there first. Everything in this book matters, but those are the seams where the gold is thickest. WHERE THE MARKS CLUSTER ember = revise these first SPEND SHORT REVISION TIME Why it matters Method is not a substitute for knowledge; it is the frame that lets your knowledge show. A candidate who knows the material but panics at the clock, or who reads past a hidden "NOT", can throw away marks they had genuinely earned. So walk in with a plan, a INTO THE EXAM rhythm for the clock, a rule to answer everything, a lawyer\'s eye for the stem, a habit of elimination, and a nose for the classic traps, and the exam becomes what it should be: simply an invitation to show what you already know. A last word You began this book with two old enemies deciding, against all the weight of history, to pool their coal and steel. You end it holding the whole architecture that grew from that decision, its institutions and its law, its freedoms and its limits, its citizens and its place in the world. That is not a small thing to carry, and you have carried it well. Read the Notice, sleep before the test, trust the story you now know by heart, and walk in steadily. The Union has been explained; the rest is yours. Good luck. The Spine of the Story THE TEST & THE FIVE HABITS The test (Notice EPSO/AD/427/26, as amended): 30 ques‐ tions · 40 minutes · pass 15/30 · in your language 2. Weight: 30% of the preliminary ranking and 25% of the final score (verbal 40/35%; digital skills 30/25%; EUFTE 15% of the final; numerical + abstract are pass-fail only). 1,490 places. Two parts since the 30 June 2026 amendment: reasoning first (expected Oct–Nov 2026); EU knowledge, digital skills and EUFTE in 2027 for those who pass. The official reference list for this test is published about two months before the test date. Five habits: (1) budget the clock, never sink; (2) answer everything (no negative marking — verify); (3) read the stem like a lawyer (NOT/EXCEPT/only/always); (4) eliminate, then choose; (5) know the trap families. HIGH-FREQUENCY ZONES Institutions & seats · the treaty timeline · primacy/direct effect & the landmark cases · competences & subsidiarity · the four freedoms · citizenship & the Charter · co-decision & QMV. If revision time is short, start here. INTO THE EXAM VERSION) 1.',
};
